window.CONTRASTS_V3 = [{"module": 6, "title": "Whole report vs partial report", "a": {"term": "Whole report", "definition": "The person sees a flashed array of letters and then names as many as they can. In Sperling's (1960) display that was 12 letters in three rows of four."}, "b": {"term": "Partial report", "definition": "A tone played after the display disappears tells the person which one row to name. They cannot know in advance which row will be cued, so they cannot memorize just one."}, "score": 8.1, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "In **whole report**, the person sees a flashed array of letters and names as many as they can. In **Sperling's (1960)** display that was **12 letters in three rows of four**, and people averaged only about **4.5 of 12 letters** (about **37.5%**). **Partial report** works differently: a **tone played after the display disappears** tells the person which one row to name.", "children": ["Whole report asks for everything, and partial report asks for one cued row."]}, {"text": "Because people cannot know in advance which row will be cued, they cannot memorize just one. Yet they averaged about **3.3 of 4 letters** (about **82%**) **whichever row was cued**. For instance, with rows like X M L T, A F N B and C D Z P, people reported only a few letters when asked for everything but nearly all of whichever row the tone picked out.", "children": ["Success on any cued row means information about all rows was available at the start."]}, {"text": "So **the whole-report limit is a limit on how much can be reported before the trace fades, not on how much was perceived**. It would be a mistake to read the 82% as proof that every letter was stored perfectly, since it is accuracy on the cued row. What matters is that the tone came after the display was gone.", "children": ["Whole report measures what survives the reporting, and partial report reveals what was there."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Immediate vs delayed partial report", "a": {"term": "Immediate partial report", "definition": "The cue tone comes right as the display ends, so the person selects the cued row while the trace is still strong. Accuracy was about 3.3 of 4 letters (about 82 percent)."}, "b": {"term": "Delayed partial report", "definition": "The cue tone is held back by an extra delay before the person selects a row. Accuracy fell to about 1 of 4 letters (25 percent)."}, "score": 8.1, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "In both conditions the person selects a row after a cue tone. In **immediate partial report** the cue comes right as the display ends, so the trace is still strong and accuracy was about **3.3 of 4 letters** (about **82%**). In **delayed partial report** the cue is held back by an extra delay, and accuracy fell to about **1 of 4 letters** (**25%**).", "children": ["The two conditions differ only in how long the person waits for the cue."]}, {"text": "Since waiting is the only thing that changes, the drop shows that the **sensory trace is fading fast**. As the cue is delayed, partial-report accuracy falls toward the **whole-report** level. In **Sperling (1960)**, the same cue that gave about 82% when immediate gave about 25% when it arrived after the delay.", "children": ["Longer delay means lower accuracy, trending toward the whole-report level."]}, {"text": "This is why **sensory memory has a large capacity but a very short duration**, with most information lost within about **one second** unless it is selected. The fall does not mean the letters were never seen. It means the representation faded before the cue could be used to select from it.", "children": ["Large capacity does not rescue information from fast decay."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Sensory memory vs short-term memory", "a": {"term": "Sensory memory", "definition": "Briefly retains incoming stimulation for seconds or fractions of a second. It has a very large capacity but fades quickly unless attention selects part of it."}, "b": {"term": "Short-term memory", "definition": "Holds about five to seven items for roughly fifteen to twenty seconds. Rehearsal can keep the contents available and move them toward long-term memory."}, "score": 8.9, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Sensory memory** and **short-term memory** are two stores of **Atkinson and Shiffrin's (1968)** modal model, and they differ in how much they hold and how long it lasts. Sensory memory briefly retains incoming stimulation for seconds or fractions of a second, with a **very large capacity**. Short-term memory holds about **five to seven items** for roughly **fifteen to twenty seconds**.", "children": ["Sensory memory holds a lot for an instant, and short-term memory holds a little for longer."]}, {"text": "**Sperling's (1960)** immediate cue let people report almost any row because so much was still in sensory memory, and a delayed cue arrived after most of it had faded. **Attention** is what moves selected information from the first store into the second. For instance, a phone directory floods sensory memory with many numbers, attention picks out the one you want, and repeating it keeps it in short-term memory long enough to dial.", "children": ["Attention is the gate between the two stores.", "Rehearsal keeps the selected item available."]}, {"text": "It would be a mistake to describe short-term memory as just a longer sensory memory. Sensory memory is large and fleeting, while short-term memory is small and depends on selection and rehearsal.", "children": ["The two stores differ in kind, not only in duration."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Icon vs echo", "a": {"term": "Icon", "definition": "The fleeting visual trace left by stimulation. It is the visual form of sensory memory, and persistence of vision (a sparkler seeming to leave a trail) shows it."}, "b": {"term": "Echo", "definition": "The brief continuation of auditory information after a sound ends. It is the auditory form of sensory memory."}, "score": 3.6, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "The **icon** is the fleeting **visual** trace left by stimulation, and the **echo** is the brief continuation of **auditory** information after a sound ends. Both are forms of **sensory memory**, so the only real difference is the sense involved: seeing for the icon and hearing for the echo.", "children": ["Icon is visual sensory memory, and echo is auditory sensory memory."]}, {"text": "**Persistence of vision**, such as a sparkler seeming to leave a trail, shows the icon at work, and **Sperling (1960)** studied iconic memory with flashed letters. For the echo, think of how you can still replay the last few words someone said for a moment after they stop.", "children": ["Each trace lingers briefly after the stimulus is gone."]}, {"text": "Both traces fade within seconds or fractions of a second, and neither is the durable representation used to remember something much later. A large capacity does not mean a long duration.", "children": ["Both are large but short-lived."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Decay vs interference", "a": {"term": "Decay", "definition": "A memory trace weakens simply because time passes. It predicts the same loss at the same delay on every trial."}, "b": {"term": "Interference", "definition": "Other memories compete with the one being tested. It predicts that loss depends on how much competing material there is, not only on time."}, "score": 10.4, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "**Decay** says a memory trace weakens simply because **time passes**, so it predicts the same loss at the same delay on every trial. **Interference** says other memories compete with the one being tested, so loss depends on **how much competing material there is**, not only on time.", "children": ["Decay depends on time alone, and interference depends on competing material."]}, {"text": "In **Brown (1958)** and **Peterson and Peterson (1959)**, participants saw **three letters and a number** and counted backward by threes for **3 to 18 seconds**, which blocks rehearsal. The average over all trials fell steadily, and Peterson and Peterson read that as decay.", "children": ["Backward counting blocks rehearsal, so forgetting could be attributed to time."]}, {"text": "But at 18 seconds recall was relatively **high on trial 1**, lower by trial 3, and **very low after many trials**, so the same delay did far more damage when more earlier sets had piled up. As presented, that trial-by-trial pattern points to **proactive interference**, which **Keppel and Underwood (1962)** offered as the alternative account. An answer that says forgetting happened because of time but ignores the trial history is describing decay when the pattern shows interference.", "children": ["Same delay, different recall, so time alone cannot explain the loss."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Retroactive vs proactive interference", "a": {"term": "Retroactive interference", "definition": "New information disrupts memory for older information. The memory that suffers is the old one, and the competitor arrived later."}, "b": {"term": "Proactive interference", "definition": "Old information disrupts memory for newer information. The memory that suffers is the new one, and the competitor arrived earlier."}, "score": 3.8, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Retroactive interference** is when **new information disrupts memory for older information**, so the memory that suffers is the old one and the competitor arrived later. **Proactive interference** is when **old information disrupts memory for newer information**, so the memory that suffers is the new one and the competitor arrived earlier.", "children": ["Retroactive harms backward in time, and proactive harms forward in time."]}, {"text": "To label a case correctly, first decide **which information the person is trying to recall**, then ask whether the competing information came before or after it. Earlier competing material is proactive and later competing material is retroactive. It would be a mistake to pick by which memory is newer in general, because the question is always the direction of harm relative to the item being recalled.", "children": ["Identify the target first, then check whether the competitor came before or after."]}, {"text": "For instance, learning a new phone number and then struggling to recall the old one is retroactive. Previous parking spots making today's spot hard to remember is proactive, and so are earlier trigrams harming recall of the current one in **Brown (1958)** and **Peterson and Peterson (1959)**. Routine events are especially vulnerable because similar memories compete, and both kinds are reduced when events are distinctive.", "children": ["Similar, routine memories compete most, and distinctiveness protects against both kinds."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Average over all trials vs trial-by-trial recall", "a": {"term": "Average over all trials", "definition": "Pools every trial together, so recall appears to drop steadily as the delay grows from 3 to 18 seconds, as though short-term memory simply decays."}, "b": {"term": "Trial-by-trial recall", "definition": "Looks at early and late trials separately. Early trials keep high recall even at 18 seconds, and later trials show very low recall at the same delay."}, "score": 8.1, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "The **average over all trials** pools every trial together, so recall appears to drop steadily as the delay grows from **3 to 18 seconds**, as though short-term memory simply decays. **Trial-by-trial recall** looks at early and late trials separately. Early trials keep **high recall even at 18 seconds**, and later trials show **very low recall at the same delay**.", "children": ["Pooling shows a smooth decline, and separating trials shows a split."]}, {"text": "In **Brown (1958)** and **Peterson and Peterson (1959)**, recall at 18 seconds was relatively high on trial one, lower by trial three, and substantially reduced after many trials. The average blends early trials with high recall and late trials with very low recall, so it hides where the steep drop actually comes from.", "children": ["Averaging blends good early trials with poor late trials."]}, {"text": "The steep drop in the average comes mostly from the later trials, where **proactive interference** has built up. Averaging therefore hides the interference behind something that looks like decay. This is why the Brown-Peterson pattern is evidence for interference and not only for time-based decay.", "children": ["The apparent decay is largely built-up interference."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Maintenance rehearsal vs elaborative rehearsal", "a": {"term": "Maintenance rehearsal", "definition": "Repeating information, such as silently saying a label or number, to prolong how long it stays available in short-term memory."}, "b": {"term": "Elaborative rehearsal", "definition": "Linking new information to knowledge already in long-term memory, such as explaining how it relates to what you know."}, "score": 4.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Maintenance rehearsal** is repeating information, such as silently saying a label or number, to prolong how long it stays available in **short-term memory**. It keeps information alive only while the repeating continues. For instance, repeating a label over and over keeps it available.", "children": ["Maintenance keeps information available only as long as you keep repeating it."]}, {"text": "**Elaborative rehearsal** is linking new information to **knowledge already in long-term memory**, such as explaining how it relates to what you know. Adding meaning supports learning that lasts longer. Explaining how that same label relates to something you already know is the elaborative version.", "children": ["Elaborative rehearsal works through meaning and connections."]}, {"text": "Repetition is not the same as understanding. If the question stresses meaning or links to existing knowledge, the answer is elaborative. **Recoding** is a third control process, which reorganizes or modifies information that is already in short-term memory.", "children": ["Repeat to hold, connect to learn, and recode to reorganize."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Miller's seven plus or minus two vs three to four items", "a": {"term": "Seven plus or minus two units", "definition": "Miller's (1956) estimate of short-term capacity, where a unit can be a digit, word, number or phrase and differs across tasks."}, "b": {"term": "About three to four items", "definition": "Alvarez and Cavanagh's (2004) estimate for visual short-term memory from change detection, where accuracy falls sharply above roughly four items."}, "score": 8.8, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Miller's (1956)** estimate of short-term capacity is **seven plus or minus two units**, where a unit can be a digit, word, number or phrase and differs across tasks. Because units can hold different amounts of information, the title does not prove that every system holds exactly seven raw items.", "children": ["Miller's figure counts units, and units vary in size."]}, {"text": "**Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)** estimated **about three to four items** for visual short-term memory using change detection. The first array stayed up for **100 milliseconds**, the second for **2,000 milliseconds**, and people judged whether the two matched. Accuracy fell sharply above roughly four items, and colored squares gave a higher estimated capacity than complex polygons or shaded cubes.", "children": ["Visual change detection gives a smaller estimate, and it depends on object complexity."]}, {"text": "The figures come from different places: **five to seven** is the modal-model estimate for short-term memory, seven plus or minus two is Miller's, and three to four is Alvarez and Cavanagh's for visual objects. Different tasks need not measure one identical capacity, so seven should not be treated as a universal limit for visual memory.", "children": ["Each number belongs to its own source and task."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Single item vs chunk", "a": {"term": "Item", "definition": "One basic unit, such as a single digit, letter or word."}, "b": {"term": "Chunk", "definition": "A larger meaningful unit built from smaller ones, such as a familiar group of digits."}, "score": 10.0, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "An **item** is one basic unit, such as a single digit, letter or word. A **chunk** is a larger meaningful unit built from smaller ones, such as a familiar group of digits. **Chunking** lets each slot in short-term memory carry more information, so the same limited number of units holds more.", "children": ["A chunk packs several items into one slot."]}, {"text": "For instance, **Ericsson and colleagues (1980)** trained a student to reach a digit span of **79** by treating strings of digits as familiar **running times**, so each running time was one chunk. The gain comes from knowledge-based organization, not from enlarging every raw limit equally.", "children": ["Familiar knowledge makes the chunks."]}, {"text": "A big span after training does not mean the memory system itself got bigger. It means each unit holds more.", "children": ["The slots stayed the same size, but each one holds more."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Episodic vs semantic memory", "a": {"term": "Episodic memory", "definition": "Memory for personal events tied to a particular time and place. Tulving described it as a kind of mental time travel."}, "b": {"term": "Semantic memory", "definition": "Memory for facts and general knowledge that is not tied to the moment you learned it."}, "score": 4.8, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Episodic memory** is memory for **personal events tied to a particular time and place**, and **Tulving** described it as a kind of mental time travel. **Semantic memory** is memory for **facts and general knowledge** that is not tied to the moment you learned it. Episodic answers 'what happened to me then', and semantic answers 'what do I know'.", "children": ["Episodic is tied to a moment, and semantic is general knowledge."]}, {"text": "For instance, remembering one particular birthday party is episodic, and knowing what a birthday is is semantic.", "children": ["The party is an event, and the concept is a fact."]}, {"text": "Both are branches of **declarative memory** inside long-term memory, and the other major branch of long-term memory is **procedural memory**, which concerns skills. They are classified by function, not by how long they last. Neither is an extra short-term store, since they sit in long-term memory.", "children": ["Both are long-term declarative memory."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Duration vs capacity", "a": {"term": "Duration", "definition": "How long information stays available in short-term memory, roughly fifteen to twenty seconds without rehearsal."}, "b": {"term": "Capacity", "definition": "How much information can be held at once, traditionally about seven plus or minus two units."}, "score": 8.7, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Duration** is how long information stays available in short-term memory, roughly **fifteen to twenty seconds** without rehearsal. **Capacity** is how much can be held at once, traditionally about **seven plus or minus two units**. A question about how long is duration, and a question about how much is capacity.", "children": ["Duration is time, and capacity is amount."]}, {"text": "Each can be changed by a strategy: **rehearsal prolongs duration**, and **chunking raises effective capacity**. Counting backward blocks rehearsal in **Brown (1958)** and **Peterson and Peterson (1959)**, so a trigram is lost within about fifteen to twenty seconds. **Ericsson and colleagues (1980)** raised a digit span to **79** by chunking.", "children": ["Rehearsal affects duration, and chunking affects capacity."]}, {"text": "So when a result changes, ask which strategy was used and not only which memory system is involved. Mixing up the two is a common slip.", "children": ["Name the strategy first, then decide which property it changed."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Short-term memory vs working memory", "a": {"term": "Short-term memory (Atkinson and Shiffrin, 1968)", "definition": "A single component that holds what has been selected for a short time. Rehearsal keeps its contents available, and the emphasis is on temporary availability."}, "b": {"term": "Working memory (Baddeley and Hitch, 1974)", "definition": "A limited-capacity system for the temporary storage and manipulation of information, with a central executive, a phonological loop and a visuospatial sketchpad."}, "score": 7.8, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Short-term memory** in the sense of **Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968)** is a single component that holds what has been selected for a short time. Rehearsal keeps its contents available, and the emphasis is on **temporary availability**. **Working memory (Baddeley and Hitch, 1974)** is a limited-capacity system for the temporary **storage and manipulation** of information.", "children": ["Short-term memory emphasizes holding, and working memory emphasizes holding and working on."]}, {"text": "The working-memory model treats memory as active: information is held and worked on at the same time. It has a **central executive**, a **phonological loop** and a **visuospatial sketchpad**, and it separates verbal and auditory information from visual and spatial information. **Baddeley** added the **episodic buffer in 2000**, so it belongs to the expanded model.", "children": ["Working memory has separate components coordinated by the executive."]}, {"text": "For instance, in 43 × 6, the 8 from 3 × 6 = 18 is held (storage) while the 1 is carried and 6 × 4 = 24 is computed (an operation). The 8 has to survive while other steps run. It would be a mistake to call working memory passive storage, since comprehension, learning and reasoning need holding and operating together.", "children": ["Storage plus operation together is the mark of working memory."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Phonological loop vs visuospatial sketchpad", "a": {"term": "Phonological loop", "definition": "Maintains verbal and auditory information, with a phonological store that holds it and an articulatory rehearsal process that repeats the sounds, even silently."}, "b": {"term": "Visuospatial sketchpad", "definition": "Holds visual information (what something looks like) and spatial information (where its parts are and how they are arranged)."}, "score": 10.7, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "The **phonological loop** maintains **verbal and auditory** information, with a **phonological store** that holds it and an **articulatory rehearsal** process that repeats the sounds, even silently. The **visuospatial sketchpad** holds **visual** information (what something looks like) and **spatial** information (where its parts are and how they are arranged).", "children": ["The loop handles sounds and words, and the sketchpad handles shapes and locations."]}, {"text": "To tell them apart, ask whether the content is sounds and words or shapes and locations. For instance, a remembered sentence uses the loop, and the remembered shape of an F uses the sketchpad. **Brooks's (1968)** opposite results show the two are at least partly independent.", "children": ["Content type decides which system is used.", "Brooks's results show the systems are partly independent."]}, {"text": "Both interact with long-term knowledge, so one calculation can use verbal rehearsal of a result together with a picture of the digits. The **central executive** is not a store for any particular kind of content. It directs and coordinates the other two.", "children": ["The executive controls the two stores and does not hold content itself."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Brooks's sentence task vs F task", "a": {"term": "Sentence task (Brooks, 1968)", "definition": "People memorized a sentence and decided for each word whether it was a noun, responding by speaking or by pointing. Pointing was easier."}, "b": {"term": "F task (Brooks, 1968)", "definition": "People memorized the shape of an F and classified each corner as inside or outside, responding by speaking or by pointing. Speaking was easier."}, "score": 9.8, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "In **Brooks's (1968)** **sentence task**, people memorized a sentence and decided for each word whether it was a noun, responding by speaking or by pointing. **Pointing was easier**. In the **F task**, people memorized the shape of an F and classified each corner as inside or outside, responding by speaking or by pointing. **Speaking was easier**.", "children": ["The sentence task favored pointing, and the F task favored speaking."]}, {"text": "In the sentence task, holding the sentence used the **phonological loop**, so speaking the answers competed with it. In the F task, following the F used the **sketchpad**, so pointing to spatially arranged responses competed with it.", "children": ["Interference appears when the response uses the same system as the held information."]}, {"text": "A task is easier when the information held and the operation performed use **different types of short-term memory**. Memorize the logic and not just the result: the easier response is the one that uses the other system.", "children": ["Hold in one system, respond with the other."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Phonological store vs articulatory rehearsal", "a": {"term": "Phonological store", "definition": "The part of the phonological loop that holds verbal and auditory information for a short time."}, "b": {"term": "Articulatory rehearsal process", "definition": "The part that keeps the contents active by repeating the sounds, even silently."}, "score": 7.0, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "The **phonological store** is the part of the phonological loop that holds verbal and auditory information for a short time. The **articulatory rehearsal process** is the part that keeps the contents active by repeating the sounds, even silently.", "children": ["The store holds the sounds, and rehearsal refreshes them."]}, {"text": "**Articulatory suppression**, speaking while trying to memorize, occupies the rehearsal process. For instance, repeating an unrelated word while trying to memorize a list leaves the rehearsal process busy. That reduces recall, and it reduces both the **word-length effect** (because short words no longer win by being quicker to rehearse) and the **phonological similarity effect** (because converting seen items into sounds is disrupted).", "children": ["Suppression blocks rehearsal and reduces effects that depend on it."]}, {"text": "Suppression does not erase the store. It blocks the process that refreshes it.", "children": ["The store is intact, but it is no longer being refreshed."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Word-length effect vs phonological similarity effect", "a": {"term": "Word-length effect", "definition": "Lists of short words are remembered better than lists of long words, because longer words take more time to say and rehearse."}, "b": {"term": "Phonological similarity effect", "definition": "Items that sound alike are confused with each other, even when they look different."}, "score": 10.1, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "The **word-length effect** is that lists of short words are remembered better than lists of long words, because longer words take more time to say and rehearse. The **phonological similarity effect** is that items that sound alike are confused with each other, even when they look different. For instance, E is confused with B because they sound alike.", "children": ["Word length concerns rehearsal time, and similarity concerns overlapping sound codes."]}, {"text": "**Baddeley and colleagues (1975, 1984)** found more short words were recalled than long words. **Ellis and Hennelly (1980)** found Welsh-English bilingual children had a shorter digit span in Welsh because Welsh number names take longer to say. Span tracks how much can be said in about **1.5 to 2 seconds**, so the language difference follows the time to say the number names and not the numbers themselves.", "children": ["Speech time, not the quantity of items, sets the span."]}, {"text": "Both effects support the idea that verbal maintenance depends on **sound-based codes** and speech-related rehearsal, and articulatory suppression reduces both.", "children": ["Both effects depend on sound codes in the loop."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Central executive vs the two storage systems", "a": {"term": "Central executive", "definition": "Directs and maintains attention, divides attention, switches it when the task changes, suppresses irrelevant information, coordinates the other components and performs calculations."}, "b": {"term": "Phonological loop and sketchpad", "definition": "Hold and rehearse particular kinds of content: verbal and auditory for the loop, visual and spatial for the sketchpad."}, "score": 9.4, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "The **central executive** is about **control**: it directs and maintains attention, divides attention, switches it when the task changes, suppresses irrelevant information, coordinates the other components and performs calculations. The **phonological loop and sketchpad** are about **content**: verbal and auditory for the loop, visual and spatial for the sketchpad.", "children": ["The executive controls, and the two storage systems hold and rehearse."]}, {"text": "For instance, following a phone number while giving directions needs the executive to split attention while the loop rehearses the digits. It would be a mistake to describe the executive as storing information.", "children": ["The executive splits attention and the loop holds the digits."]}, {"text": "Naming a controller does not explain how control is done, which is why the executive is criticized as a **homunculus**. It is today often split into **updating, shifting and inhibition** (**Miyake and colleagues, 2000**).", "children": ["Modern accounts break the executive into specific functions."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Updating vs shifting", "a": {"term": "Updating", "definition": "Changing the information currently being maintained as new information arrives."}, "b": {"term": "Shifting", "definition": "Changing the task or operation being performed."}, "score": 6.6, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Updating** is changing the information currently being maintained as new information arrives. **Shifting** is changing the task or operation being performed. **Miyake and colleagues (2000)** separate these two from **inhibition**, which suppresses information or responses that interfere with the goal.", "children": ["Updating changes contents, shifting changes operations, and inhibition blocks interference."]}, {"text": "For instance, replacing the contents you are holding with new ones is updating, switching from adding to multiplying is shifting, and ignoring a distracting number is inhibition. Updating is about the contents of memory, shifting is about the operation, and inhibition is about what to block.", "children": ["Ask whether the change is in contents, in operation, or in what gets blocked."]}, {"text": "All three are functions inside what would otherwise be one broad label, the executive.", "children": ["The three functions give the executive specific parts."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Original three-part model vs the episodic buffer", "a": {"term": "Original model", "definition": "The central executive, phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad proposed by Baddeley and Hitch (1974)."}, "b": {"term": "Episodic buffer (Baddeley, 2000)", "definition": "A fourth component of limited capacity, about four chunks, that binds information from the loop, the sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes."}, "score": 7.5, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "The **original model** has three parts proposed by **Baddeley and Hitch (1974)**: the central executive, the phonological loop and the visuospatial sketchpad. The **episodic buffer (Baddeley, 2000)** is a **fourth component** of limited capacity, about **four chunks**, that binds information from the loop, the sketchpad and long-term memory into integrated episodes. The buffer is not part of the original three-component proposal.", "children": ["The buffer is a later fourth component that binds information together."]}, {"text": "The buffer is needed because people can immediately recall about **16 words** from a meaningful sentence, more than their span for unrelated words. Meaning and long-term language knowledge let the words stay connected, so a sentence's words are kept together as connected material and not as 16 separate items.", "children": ["Meaning lets many words be held as one connected episode."]}, {"text": "Some **amnesic patients** can immediately recall a just-heard story. That suggests immediate retention of an organized episode can survive when longer-term memory is impaired.", "children": ["Immediate retention of an organized episode may survive when longer-term memory is impaired."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "High-capacity vs low-capacity participants", "a": {"term": "High-capacity participants", "definition": "Kept irrelevant blue bars out of working memory, so adding two blue distractors to two red targets changed their response little."}, "b": {"term": "Low-capacity participants", "definition": "Let the irrelevant bars in, so the same distractors raised their response toward the four-item level."}, "score": 8.0, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Vogel and colleagues (2005)** recorded **event-related potentials** during a task where **red bars** were shown for **100 milliseconds** and held across a **900 millisecond** retention interval. For **high-capacity participants**, adding two blue distractors to two red targets changed the response little, because they kept the irrelevant blue bars out of working memory. For **low-capacity participants**, the same distractors raised the response toward the four-item level, because they let the bars in.", "children": ["High-capacity people filter out distractors, and low-capacity people let them in."]}, {"text": "**Capacity was positively correlated with filtering efficiency**, so people with higher capacity are also less distracted. A lower score can reflect competing material occupying processing the targets needed.", "children": ["Capacity depends on what is kept out as well as what is held."]}, {"text": "Capacity here is not only about holding items. It is also about keeping the wrong ones out.", "children": ["Good capacity includes good filtering."]}]}, {"module": 9, "title": "Rotating a representation vs storing a fixed picture", "a": {"term": "Storing an unchanged picture", "definition": "Holding the image as it was seen, with no operation applied. If this were all that happened, deciding whether two views match would take about the same time whatever the angle between them."}, "b": {"term": "Mental rotation", "definition": "Turning an internal representation to line it up with the other view, in the picture plane or in depth. A bigger angle needs more turning, so it takes longer."}, "score": 9.5, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "If a person merely **stored an unchanged picture**, holding the image as it was seen with no operation applied, then deciding whether two views match would take about the same time whatever the angle between them. **Mental rotation** instead turns an internal representation to line it up with the other view, in the picture plane or in depth. A bigger angle needs more turning, so it takes longer.", "children": ["A fixed picture predicts no angle effect, and rotation predicts a longer time for bigger angles."]}, {"text": "**Shepard and Metzler (1971)** showed pairs of block-built objects at different orientations and asked whether they matched. For picture-plane pairs, **reaction time rose approximately linearly with the angle**, so small angles were answered faster than large ones.", "children": ["Reaction time increased steadily with the angle between the views."]}, {"text": "A static copy would predict no angle effect, so the pattern points to an **active operation on a spatial representation**. The evidence is the shape of the reaction-time curve and not what people say they felt, and the linear pattern was for picture-plane pairs.", "children": ["The reaction-time pattern is the evidence for rotation."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "States vs operators", "a": {"term": "States", "definition": "The initial state, the intermediate states along the way, and the goal state of a problem."}, "b": {"term": "Operators", "definition": "The rules that say which moves are allowed. Applying one changes the problem from one state to another."}, "score": 8.5, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**States** describe where the problem stands: the **initial state**, the **intermediate states** along the way, and the **goal state**. **Operators** are the **rules that say which moves are allowed**, and applying one changes the problem from one state to another.", "children": ["States are the positions, and operators are the moves between them."]}, {"text": "For instance, in the **Tower of Hanoi** the starting stack is the initial state and the completed stack on the target peg is the goal. The operators are **one disk at a time**, **only a disk with nothing on top**, and **never a larger disk on a smaller one**.", "children": ["Operators are the rules of legal movement, not the positions themselves."]}, {"text": "Knowing the initial and goal states does not tell you which intermediate states connect them. It would be a mistake to think that describing the finished board solves the problem, as in the numbered-tile puzzle, where you can picture the end arrangement and still not know the moves.", "children": ["Solving means finding a **sequence of operator applications** that reaches the goal.", "Stating the goal exactly is not the same as knowing the route."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Problem solving as search vs as restructuring", "a": {"term": "Search (Newell and Simon, 1956)", "definition": "Problem solving as the activity between posing a problem and reaching its solution, finding a route through possible states. Means-end analysis reduces the difference between the current state and the goal."}, "b": {"term": "Restructuring (Gestalt, Koffka, 1935)", "definition": "A perceptual view in which the way the problem is organized in the mind affects which solution is available. Restructuring reorganizes the representation so a hidden relationship becomes usable."}, "score": 10.2, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "**Search**, from **Newell and Simon (1956)**, treats problem solving as the activity between posing a problem and reaching a solution, which is finding a route through possible states. **Means-end analysis** drives it by reducing the difference between the current state and the goal.", "children": ["Search is progress through states, step by step."]}, {"text": "**Restructuring**, from the Gestalt view of **Koffka (1935)**, is a perceptual account. The way the problem is organized in the mind affects which solution is available, and restructuring **reorganizes the representation** so a hidden relationship becomes usable.", "children": ["Restructuring changes the representation so the answer appears."]}, {"text": "In **Köhler's circle problem**, for instance, the black line is one diagonal of a rectangle whose other diagonal is a radius. The two diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so **x = r**. Some problems yield to successive operations and others to a sudden reorganization, and it would be a mistake to attribute restructuring to Newell and Simon, who developed the **Logic Theorist** for the search view.", "children": ["Search and restructuring explain different routes to a solution.", "Newell and Simon belong to search, the Gestalt psychologists to restructuring."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Insight vs noninsight problems", "a": {"term": "Insight problem", "definition": "Solved by a sudden realization after the representation changes, as in the triangle and chain problems."}, "b": {"term": "Noninsight problem", "definition": "Solved by successive operations that bring the solver closer, as in algebra."}, "score": 9.5, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "An **insight problem**, such as the **triangle** and **chain** problems, is solved by a **sudden realization after the representation changes**. A **noninsight problem**, such as **algebra**, is solved by **successive operations** that bring the solver closer.", "children": ["Insight depends on a change of representation, noninsight on steady steps."]}, {"text": "**Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)** had people rate how close they felt to the solution, called warmth, **every 15 seconds**. For algebra, **warmth rose gradually** as the solution approached. For insight problems it **stayed low until close to the solution and then jumped sharply, from about 3 to 7**.", "children": ["Algebra feels like gradual progress, insight problems feel like nothing and then everything."]}, {"text": "This means people **cannot anticipate how near they are** on insight problems. It would be a mistake to think every problem is solved with an Aha experience, because some allow a methodical route.", "children": ["The sudden jump in warmth is the signature of insight."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Functional fixedness vs mental set", "a": {"term": "Functional fixedness", "definition": "Restricting an object to its familiar function."}, "b": {"term": "Mental set", "definition": "A preconceived notion about how to approach a problem, based on past experience with that problem or similar ones."}, "score": 6.8, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Functional fixedness** is **restricting an object to its familiar function**. **Mental set** is a **preconceived notion about how to approach a problem**, based on past experience with that problem or similar ones. Fixedness concerns what an object is for, and a set concerns which method keeps being applied.", "children": ["Fixedness is about an object's use, and set is about a solving method."]}, {"text": "Both are forms of **fixation**, which Gestalt psychologists saw as a major obstacle. For instance, seeing the matchbox only as a container blocks the **candle solution**, which is functional fixedness. In the **water-jug problems**, the familiar **B − A − 2C** procedure blocks the simpler **A + C** and **A − C**, which is mental set.", "children": ["Both are fixation, so the question is what is blocking the solver.", "An object blocked means fixedness, a procedure blocked means set."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Candle problem vs two-string problem", "a": {"term": "Candle problem", "definition": "Mount a candle on a corkboard so wax does not drip. The solution is to tack the matchbox to the board as a platform. Participants given empty matchboxes were twice as likely to solve it."}, "b": {"term": "Two-string problem", "definition": "Tie together two strings hanging too far apart to reach both. The solution is to tie pliers to one string as a pendulum weight. When an experimenter accidentally set a string in motion, most participants solved it."}, "score": 6.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "Both are cases of **functional fixedness**. In the **candle problem**, the task is to mount a candle on a corkboard so wax does not drip, and the box is seen as a container and not as a platform. The solution is to **tack the matchbox to the board**, and participants given **empty matchboxes were twice as likely** to solve it.", "children": ["The candle problem hides the box as a platform."]}, {"text": "In the **two-string problem**, two strings hang too far apart to reach both, and the pliers are seen as a gripping tool and not as a weight. The solution is to **tie the pliers to one string as a pendulum**. When an experimenter **accidentally set a string in motion, most participants solved it**, because a hint that restructured the representation made the string something that could swing within reach.", "children": ["The two-string problem hides the pliers as a weight.", "A hint that restructures the representation can break the fixation."]}, {"text": "In both problems the objects and the requirements stay the same. What changes is that the solver finds a different relationship among them, which means a new use for a familiar object.", "children": ["Each solution requires a new use for a familiar object."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Blank board vs bread-and-butter board", "a": {"term": "Blank or colored board", "definition": "The squares are unlabeled or only colored, so nothing points the solver toward the pairing a domino needs."}, "b": {"term": "Bread-and-butter board", "definition": "The squares are labeled bread and butter, which emphasizes the difference between the two kinds of square and the pairing a domino needs."}, "score": 8.5, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "How a problem is stated changes its difficulty. On a **blank or colored board** the squares are unlabeled or only colored, so nothing points the solver toward the pairing a domino needs. On the **bread-and-butter board** the squares are labeled, which **emphasizes the difference between the two kinds of square** and the pairing a domino needs.", "children": ["The labels make the domino's need for one of each kind easier to represent."]}, {"text": "The result was that participants in the bread-and-butter condition **solved the problem twice as fast** as those with the blank board. With a **think-aloud protocol**, solvers in that condition shifted from treating squares as positions to considering the relationship between the two kinds of square.", "children": ["Labels moved solvers from thinking about positions to thinking about relationships."]}, {"text": "The objects and the requirement did not change. Only the **representation** did, which is why the difference in difficulty points to representation and not to the problem itself.", "children": ["Same problem, different representation, different difficulty."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Total area vs pairing (mutilated checkerboard)", "a": {"term": "Total area reasoning", "definition": "62 squares matches the area of 31 dominoes, so it seems the board can be covered."}, "b": {"term": "Pairing reasoning", "definition": "Every domino covers one square of each color, and the two removed corners are the same color, so the colors are unequal."}, "score": 7.6, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Total area reasoning** notes that **62 squares matches the area of 31 dominoes**, so it seems the board can be covered. **Pairing reasoning** notes that **every domino covers one square of each color**, and that the two removed corners are the **same color**, so the colors are unequal.", "children": ["Area counts squares, and pairing counts what kinds of squares each domino needs."]}, {"text": "For instance, an ordinary board has 64 squares and 32 dominoes cover it. Remove two opposite corners and 62 squares remain, but they are **30 of one color and 32 of the other**, while 31 dominoes would need **31 of each**.", "children": ["The color counts do not match what 31 dominoes require."]}, {"text": "Equal total area does not guarantee a legal arrangement. The color imbalance applies to **every possible placement**, and moving the dominoes around cannot change it, so the covering is impossible.", "children": ["Pairs expose a constraint that area alone hides."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Goal vs subgoal", "a": {"term": "Goal", "definition": "The condition you are trying to reach, such as the whole stack moved to the third peg."}, "b": {"term": "Subgoal", "definition": "A condition that makes part of the larger task possible, not the final answer."}, "score": 6.6, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "A **goal** is the condition you are trying to reach, such as the **whole stack moved to the third peg**. A **subgoal** is a condition that makes part of the larger task possible, and it is not the final answer.", "children": ["The goal is the end condition, and a subgoal is a stepping stone toward it."]}, {"text": "**Means-end analysis** organizes search around reducing the difference between the current situation and the goal, and subgoals are the steps that do it. Different subgoals address different obstacles to the same move, one at the source and one at the destination.", "children": ["Subgoals are how means-end analysis shrinks the gap to the goal."]}, {"text": "In the **Tower of Hanoi**, for instance, the first subgoal is **freeing the large disk**, the second is **freeing the third peg**, and the third is **moving the large disk**. It would be a mistake to treat that as the answer, because the smaller disks still have to be brought over afterward.", "children": ["Reaching a subgoal does not finish the problem."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Surface features vs structural features", "a": {"term": "Surface features", "definition": "The specific elements used to describe a problem, such as a fortress, a tumor, a lightbulb, a laser or a ray."}, "b": {"term": "Structural features", "definition": "The underlying principle that governs the solution, such as a strong force that would damage the surroundings if sent through one path."}, "score": 10.1, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "**Surface features** are the specific elements used to describe a problem, such as a **fortress, a tumor, a lightbulb, a laser or a ray**. **Structural features** are the underlying principle that governs the solution, such as a **strong force that would damage the surroundings if sent through one path**.", "children": ["Surface features are what the objects are, and structure is why the solution works."]}, {"text": "Similar surface features can help a person notice a possible link, but **transfer depends on matching structure**. For instance, a fortress and a tumor do not look alike, yet they share the structure, and two lightbulb versions can keep the same surface features while changing the structure.", "children": ["Surface similarity helps noticing, but only matching structure supports transfer."]}, {"text": "In the **radiation** and **fragile-glass** problems, a strong ray would damage the surroundings on its way to an internal target, so **several low-intensity beams must converge**.", "children": ["The shared structure is a strong force that is dangerous along one path and safe when divided."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Noticing vs mapping vs applying", "a": {"term": "Noticing", "definition": "Recognizing that a source problem may be relevant to the target. It is crucial and can be the hardest step."}, "b": {"term": "Mapping and applying", "definition": "Linking the elements and relationships of the source to the target, then using that link to generate a parallel solution."}, "score": 7.6, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Gick and Holyoak** distinguished steps in using an analogy. **Noticing** is recognizing that a source problem may be relevant to the target, and it is crucial and can be the hardest step. **Mapping and applying** is linking the elements and relationships of the source to the target, then using that link to generate a parallel solution.", "children": ["Noticing is finding the source, and mapping is linking it to the target."]}, {"text": "After reading the fortress story, only about **30 percent** of participants solved the radiation problem. When told to think about the story, success rose to about **75 percent**. Having the source in memory did not guarantee spontaneous use, which points to **noticing as a major bottleneck**.", "children": ["The jump from 30 to 75 percent points to noticing as a major bottleneck."]}, {"text": "It would be a mistake to describe noticing as the same thing as mapping. For instance, in the **Russian marriage problem** mapped to the checkerboard, marriages correspond to dominoes and the two groups of people to the two square colors. Noticing is finding the source, while mapping is linking the source to the target.", "children": ["Noticing is finding the source, and mapping is linking it to the target."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Source problem vs target problem", "a": {"term": "Source problem", "definition": "A problem with relevant similarities whose solution guides a new problem."}, "b": {"term": "Target problem", "definition": "The problem currently being solved."}, "score": 6.7, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "In **analogical problem solving**, a solution is transferred from a source to a target. The **source problem** has relevant similarities and its solution guides a new problem. The **target problem** is the problem currently being solved.", "children": ["The source is what you already know, and the target is what you need to solve now."]}, {"text": "For instance, the **Russian marriage problem** is the source and the **mutilated checkerboard** is the target. In another pairing, **Duncker's radiation problem** is the source for the **lightbulb problem**.", "children": ["Source and target are roles in a given pairing, as in Russian marriage to checkerboard and radiation to lightbulb."]}, {"text": "What transfers is **the relationship among the elements, not their identical appearance**. A problem is useful as a source because of how its parts relate, not because it looks like the target.", "children": ["Relationships transfer, appearances do not need to."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Spontaneous vs prompted use of an analogy", "a": {"term": "Spontaneous use", "definition": "A source in memory is used without a hint. After just reading the fortress story, about 30 percent of participants solved the radiation problem."}, "b": {"term": "Prompted use", "definition": "The person is told to think about the story they read. Then about 75 percent solved the radiation problem."}, "score": 7.6, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Spontaneous use** means a source in memory is used without a hint. In the **fortress story**, a rebel general sends small groups along different roads so mines do not explode, and they arrive together to attack. After just reading it, only about **30 percent** solved the radiation problem.", "children": ["Without a hint, few people use the story they just read."]}, {"text": "**Prompted use** means the person is told to think about the story they read, and then about **75 percent** solved the radiation problem. The people and the story in memory were the same, and only the hint differed.", "children": ["Only the hint changed, and success more than doubled."]}, {"text": "So having the source available was not enough. The reminder made the relevant source available for mapping and application, and **noticing is a major bottleneck**. Only about **10 percent** reach the solution without a helpful analogy, so a source in memory is not the same as a source in use.", "children": ["Having a source in memory is not the same as using it."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Fragile-glass vs insufficient-intensity lightbulb sources", "a": {"term": "Fragile-glass source", "definition": "A high-intensity beam would break the glass, which matches the healthy-tissue constraint. About 69 percent who read it solved the radiation problem."}, "b": {"term": "Insufficient-intensity source", "definition": "The laser can only make low-intensity beams that cannot fuse the filament, so beams are combined, but there is no surrounding-damage constraint. About 33 percent solved it."}, "score": 7.4, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "Both are lightbulb sources, so the surface story is similar, and both use converging beams. The difference is the **structural constraint**. In the **fragile-glass source**, a high-intensity beam would break the glass, which matches the **healthy-tissue constraint** in the radiation problem.", "children": ["The fragile-glass source shares the surrounding-damage constraint with the target."]}, {"text": "In the **insufficient-intensity source**, the laser can only make **low-intensity beams that cannot fuse the filament**, so beams are combined. There is **no surrounding-damage constraint**, so the structure is weaker for the target.", "children": ["The insufficient-intensity source converges beams for a different reason."]}, {"text": "The transfer rates follow how well the underlying constraint matches the target. About **69 percent** who read the fragile-glass version solved the radiation problem, against about **33 percent** who read the insufficient-intensity version.", "children": ["The better the structural match, the more transfer: 69 versus 33 percent."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Lightbulb with vs without the radiation source", "a": {"term": "With the radiation problem as a source", "definition": "The tumor and the filament are both targets needing a strong local effect, and healthy tissue and fragile glass are both surroundings that a single strong approach would damage. About 81 percent solved the lightbulb problem."}, "b": {"term": "Without the source (control)", "definition": "About 10 percent solved the lightbulb problem."}, "score": 6.8, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "Reading the **radiation problem as a source** led about **81 percent** to solve the lightbulb problem, while the **control without the source** had about **10 percent**. The radiation problem works as a source because it shares the structure. The tumor and the filament are both targets needing a strong local effect, and healthy tissue and fragile glass are both surroundings that a single strong approach would damage.", "children": ["A source with matching structure raised success from about 10 to about 81 percent."]}, {"text": "The surface features also overlap, since both problems direct a beam toward an internal target, which makes the link easier to notice. So the match helps on both levels, in what the problems look like and in how they work.", "children": ["Overlapping surface features make the structural link easier to notice."]}, {"text": "For instance, Ruth's sealed lightbulb has a broken filament, and **several low-intensity lasers from different directions** fuse it without breaking the glass. The solution is several weak beams converging, not one strong beam.", "children": ["Weak beams converging solve both problems."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Experts vs novices organizing problems", "a": {"term": "Experts", "definition": "Physics professors who sorted problems by structural features, such as conservation of energy."}, "b": {"term": "Novices", "definition": "Students with one semester of physics who sorted problems by surface features, such as inclined planes."}, "score": 9.5, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "In **Chi et al. (1982)**, physics professors and students sorted **24 physics problems** into groups by similarity. **Experts**, the professors, sorted by **structural features** such as conservation of energy. **Novices**, students with one semester of physics, sorted by **surface features** such as inclined planes.", "children": ["Experts sort by principle, and novices sort by appearance."]}, {"text": "The same problem **24** got a different partner under the two organizations. Novices paired **23 and 24** because both have inclines. Experts paired **21 and 24** through conservation of energy, even though 21 has a block and spring on a horizontal surface.", "children": ["One problem was grouped differently depending on what counted as similar."]}, {"text": "Expertise changes which relationship is the basis for similarity. Novices go by what the problem looks like, and experts go by the principle that solves it.", "children": ["Knowledge reshapes what a person sees as similar."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Meaningful vs random chess positions", "a": {"term": "Actual game positions", "definition": "A master sees familiar groups of pieces and recalls each group as one chunk drawn from long-term memory."}, "b": {"term": "Random arrangements", "definition": "The same kinds of pieces on the same board with no meaningful relationships, so there are no familiar chunks and the master must remember piece by piece."}, "score": 10.1, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "In **Chase and Simon (1973)**, a chess master and a beginner with **fewer than 100 hours** of experience each had **five seconds** with the board. The master was much better on **actual game positions**, where familiar groups of pieces are each recalled as **one chunk drawn from long-term memory**.", "children": ["On real positions the master recalls chunks, not single pieces."]}, {"text": "On **random arrangements**, the same kinds of pieces sit on the same board with no meaningful relationships. There are no familiar chunks, so the master must remember piece by piece, and the **advantage was greatly reduced**.", "children": ["Without meaning there are no chunks, and the master's edge shrinks."]}, {"text": "Only the meaning of the arrangement changed, so the edge comes from **organized domain knowledge**. It would be a mistake to say a chess master has a better memory in general.", "children": ["The master's memory advantage is specific to meaningful chess positions."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Comparing sources vs using one source", "a": {"term": "Using one source", "definition": "Applying a single familiar story or problem to a new target, so what you notice depends on how much the target looks like that source."}, "b": {"term": "Analogical encoding", "definition": "Comparing two sources and working out what they share, for example two stories in which separate weak forces converge on one target."}, "score": 6.4, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "With **one source**, you apply a single familiar story or problem to a new target, and what you notice depends on how much the target looks like that source. The structure stays buried in that story's details.", "children": ["One source leaves structure tangled up with details."]}, {"text": "**Analogical encoding** means **comparing two sources** and working out what they share, for example two stories in which separate weak forces converge on one target. Because the details do not match, what is left in common is the **structure**, which directs reasoning toward structural features.", "children": ["Comparison strips away details and leaves the shared structure."]}, {"text": "A later target can then be approached through the common relationship and not through one familiar situation. For instance, compare the fortress story with a second convergence story, then try the radiation problem. It would be a mistake to treat analogical encoding as being reminded of one source, since it is about comparing sources.", "children": ["The benefit comes from the comparison, not from a single reminder."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Laboratory analogies vs in vivo analogies", "a": {"term": "Laboratory tasks", "definition": "Researchers give a source and a target under controlled conditions, and people often fail to use the analogy on their own, as when the fortress story helped only about 30 percent."}, "b": {"term": "In vivo problem solving", "definition": "Researchers observe people solving real problems in their ordinary setting, where analogies are used routinely."}, "score": 6.4, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "In **laboratory tasks**, researchers give a source and a target under controlled conditions, and people often fail to use the analogy on their own. Fortress to radiation gave only about **30 percent** unprompted. In **in vivo** problem solving, researchers observe people solving real problems in their ordinary setting, where analogies are used routinely, and scientists use about **3 to 15 analogies** in a one-hour laboratory meeting.", "children": ["Analogies are often not used spontaneously in the laboratory and are used routinely at work."]}, {"text": "This is the **analogical paradox**: analogies are hard to use in the laboratory and routine in real work. It is a contrast in how often analogies are used, not a contradiction in the theory.", "children": ["The paradox describes a gap in usage, not a flaw in the account."]}, {"text": "Each method has a cost. **In vivo research** keeps the ordinary context but is time-consuming and gives up control of variables, while **laboratory work** isolates variables but removes the context.", "children": ["In vivo trades control for context, and laboratory work trades context for control."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Expert strengths vs expert limits", "a": {"term": "Within the field", "definition": "An expert is extremely knowledgeable after a very long time learning and practicing, perhaps 10,000 to 20,000 hours for chess. Experts solve problems in their field faster and more successfully than beginners and spend more time analyzing before proceeding."}, "b": {"term": "Outside the field and in new situations", "definition": "The advantage is specific to the field, so experts are no better than novices outside it. Too much knowledge can lock in a familiar approach and make the expert less open to new ways of looking at a problem."}, "score": 4.4, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "Within the field, an expert is extremely knowledgeable after a very long time learning and practicing, perhaps **10,000 to 20,000 hours for chess**. Experts solve problems in their field **faster and more successfully** than beginners and spend more time analyzing before proceeding.", "children": ["Inside the field, experts are faster, more successful and more deliberate."]}, {"text": "Outside the field and in new situations, the advantage is **specific to the field**, so experts are no better than novices outside it. Too much knowledge can lock in a familiar approach and make the expert **less open to new ways** of looking at a problem. Expert does not mean better at everything.", "children": ["The expert's edge does not travel beyond the field."]}, {"text": "The same knowledge causes both. It lets experts recognize the principle behind a problem, as when physics professors in **Chi et al. (1982)** sorted problems by principle. But when a problem needs the usual procedure dropped, the knowledge can act like a **mental set**, as when a familiar procedure dominates in the water-jug problems.", "children": ["Knowledge is a strength for recognizing principles and a risk when a procedure must be dropped."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Availability vs representativeness heuristic", "a": {"term": "Availability heuristic", "definition": "Judging how likely something is by how easily examples come to mind."}, "b": {"term": "Representativeness heuristic", "definition": "Judging whether A belongs to class B by how much A resembles B."}, "score": 7.6, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "Both are **heuristics**, which are **rapid rules of thumb** that are likely to give a correct answer but are not foolproof. What separates them is **what supplies the shortcut**. With the **availability heuristic**, the judgment comes from **remembered examples**, while with the **representativeness heuristic** it comes from **similarity to a type**.", "children": ["Ask whether the answer rests on memory for examples or on resemblance to a category."]}, {"text": "For instance, a **botulism death in the news** makes it seem likelier than asthma. That is availability. When people hear that Robert resembles a librarian and then say he is a librarian, they are judging by how well he matches the type, which is representativeness.", "children": ["Easy recall of cases signals availability, and a good match to a stereotype signals representativeness."]}, {"text": "It would be a mistake to treat an easily remembered event as a common one. Memorability is also affected by **distinctiveness** and **emotion**, and neither of those makes an event more frequent.", "children": ["Memorable does not mean common."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Base rate vs description information", "a": {"term": "Base rate", "definition": "The background prevalence of categories, such as 70 lawyers and 30 engineers, or about 2.2 million farms and 120,000 libraries."}, "b": {"term": "Description information", "definition": "Personal characteristics that resemble a category, such as Jack's hobbies or Robert's glasses and quiet speech."}, "score": 6.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "The **base rate** is the background prevalence of the categories, such as **70 lawyers and 30 engineers**, or about **2.2 million farms and 120,000 libraries**. **Description information** is a personal characteristic that resembles a category, such as Jack's hobbies or Robert's glasses and quiet speech.", "children": ["One tells you how common each category is, and the other tells you how much a person resembles one."]}, {"text": "People use the base rate correctly when it is all they have. With only the **70/30 split**, they say there is a **30 in 100** chance the person is an engineer. Once Jack is described as careful and enjoying mathematical puzzles, they call him an engineer, which is **base-rate neglect**, where similarity displaces the prevalence of the categories.", "children": ["The base rate is used when alone and dropped once a description arrives."]}, {"text": "It would be a mistake to follow the description alone. The base rate favors **farmer** for Robert even though he fits the librarian description.", "children": ["A good fit to a description does not outweigh how many people are in each category."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Conjunction rule vs representativeness", "a": {"term": "Conjunction rule", "definition": "Two things together can never be more probable than either alone, because every case where both are true is also a case where each is true."}, "b": {"term": "Representativeness", "definition": "Judging by how well a description fits a typical case, so a more detailed story that fits the person better feels more probable."}, "score": 7.6, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "The **conjunction rule** says two things together can **never be more probable than either alone**, because every case where both are true is also a case where each is true. **Representativeness** is a shortcut that judges by how well a description fits a typical case, so a more detailed story that fits the person better feels more probable.", "children": ["One is a rule about nested sets, and the other is a shortcut by resemblance."]}, {"text": "They conflict when the combined description fits better than its single part. Linda is described as single, outspoken, bright, trained in philosophy and active in antinuclear demonstrations, and about **82 percent** chose that she is a bank teller and active in the feminist movement over bank teller alone. But feminist bank tellers are a **subset** of bank tellers, so the combination cannot be more probable.", "children": ["The 82 percent choice follows fit and breaks the conjunction rule."]}, {"text": "More detail can make a story fit better, but it never makes it more probable.", "children": ["Fit and probability are different things."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Number of winners vs probability of winning", "a": {"term": "Attending to the number of favorable items", "definition": "Seven red beans looks like more chances than one red bean."}, "b": {"term": "Using the probability", "definition": "Seven in 100 is 7 percent and one in 10 is 10 percent, so the bowl with fewer red beans is better."}, "score": 7.5, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "Attending to the **number of favorable items** means that **seven red beans** looks like more chances than one red bean. Using the **probability** means seeing that seven in 100 is **7 percent** and one in 10 is **10 percent**, so the bowl with fewer red beans is better.", "children": ["Counts can point one way while proportions point the other."]}, {"text": "A larger number of favorable items draws attention away from the **denominator**, the total number of possible draws. The decision then depends on considerations other than the actual probabilities of winning. In the jellybean problem a participant earns one dollar for each red bean drawn, and most participants chose the **7-out-of-100 bowl** over the **1-out-of-10 bowl**.", "children": ["Big numerators distract from the denominator, and the worse bowl wins."]}, {"text": "The safe habit is to compare proportions, not counts.", "children": ["Ask how many out of how many."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Small sample vs large sample", "a": {"term": "Larger hospital", "definition": "About 45 births a day, so its daily proportion of boys is more stable."}, "b": {"term": "Smaller hospital", "definition": "About 15 births a day, so extreme proportions are more likely."}, "score": 6.8, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "In the hospital problem, the larger hospital has about **45 births a day** and the smaller has about **15**. Half of all babies are boys, but a single day's 15 births swing more than 45 births, so the smaller hospital is more likely to show extreme proportions.", "children": ["Smaller samples vary more around the true proportion."]}, {"text": "The correct answer is the **smaller hospital** for more days with over 60 percent boys. The most common response, chosen by **56 percent**, was **about the same**. The **law of large numbers** says larger random samples become more representative, and people wrongly expect small samples to be representative too.", "children": ["Most people give the same answer for both because they expect every sample to look like the population."]}, {"text": "Do not confuse the absolute number of boys with how often an extreme proportion occurs. The larger hospital has more boys in total, but its daily proportion is more stable.", "children": ["The question is about proportions, not totals."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Confirmation bias vs myside bias", "a": {"term": "Confirmation bias", "definition": "Seeking information that fits a hypothesis and overlooking what argues against it."}, "b": {"term": "Myside bias", "definition": "Generating and evaluating evidence, and testing hypotheses, in ways biased toward one's own opinions and attitudes."}, "score": 9.1, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "**Confirmation bias** is seeking information that fits a hypothesis and overlooking what argues against it. **Myside bias** is generating and evaluating evidence, and testing hypotheses, in ways biased toward **one's own opinions and attitudes**.", "children": ["Myside bias is confirmation bias with the hypothesis being one's own opinion."]}, {"text": "The two are not rival biases. Myside bias is **one form of confirmation bias**, tied to one's own opinions. The **2, 4, 6 task** shows confirmation bias in general, while the capital punishment article shows myside bias.", "children": ["Use the 2, 4, 6 task for confirmation bias and the capital punishment study for myside bias."]}, {"text": "In Lord and colleagues (1979), people **for and against capital punishment** read the **same article**, and each side interpreted it as favoring their own position.", "children": ["Identical evidence was read as support by both sides."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Testing what fits vs trying to disconfirm (2, 4, 6)", "a": {"term": "Positive testing", "definition": "Writing sets that fit the hypothesis, such as increasing by two, which keeps returning yes."}, "b": {"term": "Disconfirming", "definition": "Testing the boundaries of the belief to find where it breaks."}, "score": 9.1, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "In the 2, 4, 6 task, participants write their own sets and get feedback only on whether each fits. With **positive testing**, they write sets that fit their hypothesis, such as **increasing by two**, and the feedback keeps returning yes.", "children": ["Positive testing produces a steady stream of yes answers."]}, {"text": "The most common hypothesis is **increasing intervals of two**, but the actual rule is **any three numbers in increasing order**. **Disconfirming** means testing the boundaries of the belief to find where it breaks, and only that reveals that the narrow belief is wrong.", "children": ["Only attempts to break the hypothesis expose that it is too narrow."]}, {"text": "It would be a mistake to treat repeated yes feedback as proof. Yes feedback does not confirm a rule that is too narrow.", "children": ["Many confirmations do not show the rule is right."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Taboo tradeoff vs tragic tradeoff", "a": {"term": "Taboo tradeoff", "definition": "Mixes sacred and secular domains, such as a child's life against money. It provokes moral outrage."}, "b": {"term": "Tragic tradeoff", "definition": "Compares two sacred values with each other. People are less morally outraged."}, "score": 10.0, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "A **taboo tradeoff** mixes **sacred and secular domains**, such as a child's life against money, and it provokes **moral outrage**. A **tragic tradeoff** compares **two sacred values** with each other, and people are less morally outraged.", "children": ["Sacred against secular is taboo, and sacred against sacred is tragic."]}, {"text": "The **Sacred Value Protection Model** (Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green and Lerner, 2000) says sacred values are **compartmentalized** from secular ones. So ask whether the competing item is a secular benefit, which makes it taboo, or another sacred value, which makes it tragic.", "children": ["The test is the type of the competing item."]}, {"text": "For instance, Johnny's liver costing **one million dollars** is taboo, while **two children and one available liver** is tragic. A tragic tradeoff still involves a hard choice, but it does not threaten the sacred-secular boundary.", "children": ["Tragic choices are hard but do not cross the boundary."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Outrage by tradeoff type and decision (Tetlock and colleagues, 2000)", "a": {"term": "Taboo tradeoff", "definition": "A sacred value (Johnny's life) is weighed against a secular benefit (one million dollars). Not saving Johnny drew much greater outrage than saving him, and spending the money on other uses instead of the child received the strongest negative response."}, "b": {"term": "Tragic tradeoff", "definition": "Two sacred values compete (two children, one available liver). Outrage was similar whether Johnny was saved or not, because either choice leaves a sacred concern unmet."}, "score": 10.0, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "Tetlock and colleagues (2000) rated **moral outrage** for each combination of tradeoff type and decision. The finding is an **interaction**, because how outraged people are depends on both the type of value conflict and the decision made within it.", "children": ["Neither the tradeoff type nor the decision alone predicts outrage."]}, {"text": "In the **taboo tradeoff**, a sacred value (Johnny's life) is weighed against a secular benefit (one million dollars). **Not saving Johnny drew much greater outrage than saving him**, and spending the money on other uses instead of the child received the **strongest negative response**.", "children": ["In the taboo case the decision matters a great deal."]}, {"text": "In the **tragic tradeoff**, two sacred values compete (two children, one available liver). **Outrage was similar whether Johnny was saved or not**, because either choice leaves a sacred concern unmet.", "children": ["In the tragic case the decision barely changes the outrage."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Moral absolutists vs non-absolutists", "a": {"term": "Moral absolutists", "definition": "Treat the issue as a sacred value and reject compromise regardless of benefit."}, "b": {"term": "Non-absolutists", "definition": "Have strong preferences against compromise but do not treat the issue as sacred."}, "score": 8.5, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Moral absolutists** treat the issue as a **sacred value** and reject compromise regardless of benefit. **Non-absolutists** have strong preferences against compromise but do not treat the issue as sacred.", "children": ["The difference is whether the issue is sacred, not how strongly it is felt."]}, {"text": "The way to tell them apart is to ask whether **benefits can enter the decision at all**. In Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), who studied **601 Israeli settlers, 535 Palestinian refugees and 719 Palestinian students**, adding money **increased** anger, disgust and support for violence among absolutists. For non-absolutists it **reduced** them.", "children": ["Money backfires for absolutists and helps for non-absolutists."]}, {"text": "It would be a mistake to call absolutists simply people who feel strongly. Their issue is sacred.", "children": ["Intensity of feeling does not make someone an absolutist."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Money vs symbolic recognition", "a": {"term": "Material incentive (Taboo+)", "definition": "Money added to the deal, such as one billion dollars a year for ten years."}, "b": {"term": "Symbolic recognition", "definition": "Israeli recognition of the historic and legitimate right of Palestinians to their own state and an apology for wrongs done."}, "score": 8.8, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Money** changes the economic benefit of a deal, while **symbolic recognition** addresses the status of a group's claim. Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007) added to the deal either **one billion dollars a year for ten years** (the material incentive, Taboo+) or **Israeli recognition of the historic and legitimate right of Palestinians to their own state and an apology for wrongs done**.", "children": ["One adds material benefit, and the other speaks to status and wrongs done."]}, {"text": "Money **raised absolutists' anger**. The symbolic offer was associated with **lower anger and lower support for violence**.", "children": ["For absolutists, money inflames and symbolic recognition calms."]}, {"text": "Material benefit and symbolic reciprocity are different kinds of offer.", "children": ["Treat them as separate things, not as bigger and smaller versions of one offer."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Incentive (Taboo+) vs disincentive (Taboo−)", "a": {"term": "Incentive", "definition": "A benefit offered for agreeing, such as 40 billion dollars from the United Nations."}, "b": {"term": "Disincentive", "definition": "A cost imposed for refusing, such as the threat of additional sanctions."}, "score": 10.1, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "An **incentive** is a benefit offered for agreeing, such as **40 billion dollars from the United Nations**. A **disincentive** is a cost imposed for refusing, such as the **threat of additional sanctions**. Incentives change the benefit of accepting, and disincentives change the cost of refusing.", "children": ["One sweetens acceptance, and the other punishes refusal."]}, {"text": "Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010) asked Iranian participants about a deal to give up their nuclear program, with or without an incentive or a sanction. Participants with a **sacred value** showed **lower approval for energy with both the incentive and the sanction** than with the baseline.", "children": ["Both kinds of offer lowered approval among those with a sacred value."]}, {"text": "For a sacred commitment, improving the material balance does not improve acceptability.", "children": ["Shifting costs and benefits cannot buy a sacred value."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Participants with vs without a sacred value (Dehghani)", "a": {"term": "With a sacred value", "definition": "Said giving up the program should not be done no matter the consequences. About 14 percent for energy and 13 percent for weapons."}, "b": {"term": "Without a sacred value", "definition": "Had much higher approval and lower anger, with little variation across conditions."}, "score": 10.1, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "Dehghani, Atran and colleagues (2010) and Dehghani, Iliev and colleagues (2009) used a measure based on Baron and Spranca (1997). Participants **with a sacred value** said giving up the program should not be done **no matter the consequences**, about **14 percent for energy and 13 percent for weapons**.", "children": ["Only a small share held the program as sacred."]}, {"text": "For energy, **anticipated anger was highest with the incentive** and **elevated with sanctions** for those holding a sacred value. Participants **without a sacred value** had much higher approval and lower anger, with little variation across conditions, so for them the deals barely mattered.", "children": ["The deal terms changed reactions for the sacred group and hardly at all for the rest."]}, {"text": "The sacred group was small, but it responded differently from everyone else.", "children": ["A small group can show a distinct pattern."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Net lives saved vs reluctance to cause a loss", "a": {"term": "Net-count reasoning", "definition": "Choosing the option that saves more overall. Rerouting the convoy saves 1,000 at a second camp and costs 100 at the first, a net of 900."}, "b": {"term": "Reluctance to cause a loss", "definition": "Resisting an action that makes others die even when more are saved elsewhere. In Ritov and Baron (1999), 63 percent did not choose to reroute."}, "score": 10.7, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "**Net-count reasoning** means choosing the option that saves more overall. In the convoy dilemma, food trucks are traveling to a refugee camp during a famine, and rerouting the convoy saves **1,000** at a second camp and costs **100** at the first, a **net of 900**.", "children": ["A net count says reroute."]}, {"text": "**Reluctance to cause a loss** is resisting an action that makes others die even when more are saved elsewhere. In Ritov and Baron (1999), **63 percent** did not choose to reroute. Counting treats the lives lost by acting and the lives lost by not acting the same, but many people do not.", "children": ["Many people weigh harm caused by acting more heavily than harm allowed."]}, {"text": "Some would keep the original route even if only **one person** at the first camp would die. The better net count did not decide the choice.", "children": ["Net lives saved did not drive the decision."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Expected utility theory vs real choices", "a": {"term": "Expected utility theory", "definition": "Assumes rational people with all relevant information choose the option with the highest expected utility, which combines how valuable each outcome is with its chance. The monetary version uses the highest expected payoff."}, "b": {"term": "Real choices", "definition": "People find value in things other than money, and many decisions do not maximize the probability of the best outcome."}, "score": 3.8, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Expected utility theory** assumes rational people with all relevant information choose the option with the **highest expected utility**, which combines how valuable each outcome is with its chance. The monetary version uses the highest expected payoff. It gives a procedure for the best choice.", "children": ["The theory tells you what a maximizer would pick."]}, {"text": "Real people are not **utility-maximization machines**. They find value in things other than money, and many decisions do not maximize the probability of the best outcome. For instance, people choose casino gambling despite knowing the odds are strongly against them.", "children": ["The theory describes a maximizer, not what people actually do."]}, {"text": "Utility is how desirable an outcome is to the person, which is not the same as money.", "children": ["Utility is personal value, not dollars."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Valid vs true", "a": {"term": "Valid", "definition": "The conclusion follows logically from the premises. It depends on the form of the reasoning."}, "b": {"term": "True", "definition": "The statements match the world."}, "score": 8.5, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Validity** and **truth** answer two separate questions. Validity asks whether the conclusion **follows logically from the premises**, so it depends on the **form** of the reasoning. Truth asks whether the statements **match the world**.", "children": ["Validity is about form, truth is about the world."]}, {"text": "The two can come apart. For instance, \"All birds are animals, all animals have four legs, therefore all birds have four legs\" is **valid**, but its conclusion is **false** because premise 2 is false. It would be a mistake to think a valid argument guarantees a true conclusion, and the reverse also holds, since an invalid argument can have a true conclusion.", "children": ["A valid argument can have a false conclusion, and an invalid one can have a true conclusion.", "Ask separately: does it follow, and are the statements correct?"]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Modus ponens vs modus tollens", "a": {"term": "Modus ponens", "definition": "If p then q; p; therefore q. Valid, and 97 percent judged it correctly."}, "b": {"term": "Modus tollens", "definition": "If p then q; not q; therefore not p. Valid, but only 60 percent judged it correctly."}, "score": 7.9, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "In **modus ponens** you start with if p then q, affirm **p**, and conclude **q**. In **modus tollens** you deny **q** and conclude **not p**. Both forms are **valid**.", "children": ["Ponens affirms p to get q, tollens denies q to get not p."]}, {"text": "They are not judged equally, though. **97 percent** judged modus ponens correctly, but only **60 percent** judged modus tollens correctly, so tollens feels harder. For instance, \"If I study I'll get a good grade; I didn't get a good grade; therefore I didn't study\" is valid, even though it feels less natural.", "children": ["Same validity, but 97 percent versus 60 percent correct."]}, {"text": "Both valid forms were still judged correctly more often than both invalid forms. So tollens is harder than ponens, but it is not in the same trouble as an invalid form.", "children": ["Valid forms beat invalid forms in accuracy, even the harder one."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Modus tollens vs syllogism 3", "a": {"term": "Modus tollens", "definition": "From if p then q, plus not q, you may conclude not p. This form is valid."}, "b": {"term": "Syllogism 3", "definition": "From if p then q, plus q, you may not conclude p. This form is not valid, and only 40 percent judged it correctly."}, "score": 7.9, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Valid forms** move from p to q, or from not q to not p. **Modus tollens** is the second kind, so it is valid. **Syllogism 3** goes from q back to p, which is **not valid**, and only **40 percent** judged it correctly.", "children": ["Valid forms run p to q or not q to not p, never q back to p."]}, {"text": "Going from q back to p fails because there may be other ways to get q. For instance, \"If I live in Tucson I live in Arizona; I live in Arizona; therefore I live in Tucson\" does not follow. The first premise says what follows from being in Tucson, and it does not say that Tucson is the only way to be in Arizona.", "children": ["Other routes to q mean q cannot send you back to p.", "The premise says what follows from p, not that p is the only route."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Syllogism 3 vs syllogism 4", "a": {"term": "Syllogism 3", "definition": "If p then q; q; therefore p. Not valid."}, "b": {"term": "Syllogism 4", "definition": "If p then q; not p; therefore not q. Not valid."}, "score": 7.9, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Syllogism 3** and **syllogism 4** are both **not valid**, and **40 percent** judged each one correctly. They differ only in the second premise: in 3 it is **q**, and in 4 it is **not p**.", "children": ["Same invalidity and accuracy; the second premise is what differs."]}, {"text": "Syllogism 4 reads: if p then q; not p; therefore not q. The first premise says nothing about what happens when someone does not study. For instance, \"If I study I'll get a good grade; I did not study; so I did not get a good grade\" does not follow, because a good grade could come another way.", "children": ["Denying p says nothing about q, since q can arise another way."]}, {"text": "It would be a mistake to confuse these two with the valid forms that use the same premises.", "children": ["Do not mistake an invalid form for its valid look-alike."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Inductive vs deductive reasoning", "a": {"term": "Inductive reasoning", "definition": "Reasoning from observations to conclusions that are probably but not definitely true."}, "b": {"term": "Deductive reasoning", "definition": "Determining whether a conclusion logically follows from the premises."}, "score": 3.6, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Inductive reasoning** goes from observations to conclusions that are **probably but not definitely true**. Its strength depends on the **representativeness**, **number** and **quality** of the observations. For instance, many white swans support the expectation that the next swan is white, and one clearly observed black swan contradicts it.", "children": ["Induction gives probable conclusions that new evidence can overturn."]}, {"text": "**Deductive reasoning** asks something different, which is whether a conclusion **logically follows** from the premises. It does not ask whether the conclusion is probable. For instance, checking whether a conclusion follows from two premises is deductive, and the answer is valid or invalid.", "children": ["Deduction yields valid or invalid, not probable."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Abstract vs concrete Wason problem", "a": {"term": "Abstract version", "definition": "Cards E, K, 4, 7 with the rule vowel then even number. The correct cards are E and 7. 46 percent chose the 4 and only 7 percent chose the 7."}, "b": {"term": "Concrete version", "definition": "The rule is 'If drinking beer, then 21 years old', with cards Beer, Soda, 16 years old and 21 years old. The correct cards are beer and 16 years old, and 73 percent answered correctly."}, "score": 8.1, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "Both versions of the Wason problem have the same **if p then q** form. In the **abstract version**, the cards are E, K, 4, 7 and the rule is vowel then even number. The correct cards are **E and 7**, yet **46 percent** chose the 4 and only **7 percent** chose the 7.", "children": ["Abstract content: people mostly picked the wrong cards."]}, {"text": "In the **concrete version** the rule is \"If drinking beer, then 21 years old,\" with cards Beer, Soda, 16 years old and 21 years old. The correct cards are **beer and 16 years old**, and **73 percent** answered correctly. Familiar content calls up a **permission schema**, which makes the **falsification principle** easy to apply: look for situations that falsify the rule, and someone drinking beer while under 21 is the only case that breaks it.", "children": ["Same logical form, but 73 percent correct with concrete content.", "Falsify the rule by looking for the case that breaks it."]}, {"text": "Context matters, but it would be a mistake to say familiarity alone is the reason for the improvement.", "children": ["The permission schema, not familiarity alone, helps."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Permission schema vs plain familiarity", "a": {"term": "Permission schema", "definition": "Stored knowledge that if a condition is satisfied, an action may be carried out, for example that being 21 permits drinking beer. It leads you to check for anyone doing the action without the condition."}, "b": {"term": "Plain familiarity", "definition": "Simply having experience with the content. By itself it does not always improve reasoning."}, "score": 6.4, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "A **permission schema** is stored knowledge that if a condition is satisfied, an action may be carried out. For instance, being 21 is the condition and drinking beer is what can then be carried out. It leads you to check for anyone doing the action **without the condition**.", "children": ["A permission schema tells you to hunt for action without the condition."]}, {"text": "**Plain familiarity** is simply having experience with the content, and by itself it does not always improve reasoning. The improvement therefore does not come from familiarity alone. A permission rule is easier to reason about than an abstract rule about letters and numbers because the setting calls up the schema.", "children": ["Familiarity alone is not the cause of the improvement.", "The setting helps when it calls up a permission schema."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Gain frame vs loss frame", "a": {"term": "Gain frame", "definition": "Outcomes are described as people saved. Program A saves 200 for certain, while Program B has a 1/3 chance that all 600 are saved and a 2/3 chance that none are. 72 percent chose the sure A, a risk-averse choice."}, "b": {"term": "Loss frame", "definition": "The same outcomes are described as people who die. Program C means 400 die for certain, while Program D has a 1/3 chance that no one dies and a 2/3 chance that 600 die. 78 percent chose the gamble D, a risk-taking choice."}, "score": 10.1, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "In the **gain frame**, Program A saves **200 for certain**, while Program B has a **1/3 chance** that all 600 are saved and a **2/3 chance** that none are. **72 percent** chose the sure A, a risk-averse choice. In the **loss frame**, Program C means **400 die for certain**, while Program D has a **1/3 chance** that no one dies and a **2/3 chance** that 600 die. **78 percent** chose the gamble D, a risk-taking choice.", "children": ["Gain wording pushed people to the sure option, loss wording to the gamble."]}, {"text": "Program A is the same outcome as C, and B is the same gamble as D, so only the **wording** changed (Tversky and Kahneman, 1981). In the gain frame people avoid the uncertain option, and in the loss frame they accept uncertainty in the hope of avoiding a certain loss. With 600 people expected to die, the same facts led to opposite choices.", "children": ["Same facts, different wording, opposite choice.", "Loss framing makes people accept risk to avoid a sure loss."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Opt-in vs opt-out", "a": {"term": "Opt-in", "definition": "You must take a step to be an organ donor. Consent is under 28 percent."}, "b": {"term": "Opt-out", "definition": "You are a donor unless you request otherwise. Consent is over 99 percent."}, "score": 7.7, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "Under **opt-in**, you must take a step to be an organ donor, and consent is **under 28 percent**. Under **opt-out**, you are a donor unless you request otherwise, and consent is **over 99 percent**.", "children": ["Opt-in gives under 28 percent consent, opt-out over 99 percent."]}, {"text": "The easiest way to see the difference is to ask what doing nothing gives you. Doing nothing leaves you a non-donor under opt-in and a donor under opt-out. The **status quo bias** is the tendency to do nothing when faced with a difficult decision, so the **default decides the result**. The rates differ because of the default, and it would be a mistake to read them as a difference in underlying attitude.", "children": ["Doing nothing means a different outcome under each default.", "Status quo bias lets the default decide, not attitude."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Expected emotions vs incidental emotions", "a": {"term": "Expected emotions", "definition": "Emotions people predict they will feel about an outcome."}, "b": {"term": "Incidental emotions", "definition": "Emotions not specifically related to the decision, from disposition, recent experience or surroundings."}, "score": 10.2, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "**Expected emotions** are the emotions people predict they will feel about an outcome. They are predictions about the outcome itself. Kermer and colleagues (2006) studied predicted feelings about a coin flip.", "children": ["Expected emotions concern the outcome being decided."]}, {"text": "**Incidental emotions** are not specifically related to the decision. They come from disposition, recent experience or surroundings, and yet they still change the decision. Lerner and colleagues (2004) induced mood with a film clip. A person's general disposition also counts as an incidental source, so it can change a decision even when it has nothing to do with the choice.", "children": ["Incidental emotions come from something unrelated to the choice.", "Even unrelated emotions can change the decision."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Predicted vs actual feelings (Kermer, 2006)", "a": {"term": "Predicted feelings", "definition": "Participants expected losing $3 to lower happiness about 4 points and winning $5 to raise it about 2."}, "b": {"term": "Actual feelings", "definition": "The actual drop from losing was about 1 point and the rise from winning about 1.2."}, "score": 9.5, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "Kermer and colleagues (2006) gave participants **$5** and a coin flip to win $5 more or lose $3. **Predicted feelings** were that losing $3 would lower happiness about **4 points** and winning $5 would raise it about **2**.", "children": ["Predictions: a big drop from losing, a modest rise from winning."]}, {"text": "The **actual feelings** were much milder. The drop from losing was about **1 point**, and the rise from winning about **1.2**. People overestimate the negative feeling because they do not consider the **coping mechanisms** that soften a loss.", "children": ["The real loss hurt far less than predicted.", "Ignoring coping makes a loss seem worse than it feels."]}, {"text": "That feeds **risk aversion**, because people believe a loss will have a greater impact than a gain of the same size.", "children": ["Overpredicted loss impact pushes people toward avoiding risk."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Sadness vs disgust: buying and selling prices (Lerner, 2004)", "a": {"term": "Sadness", "definition": "Lowered the selling price to about $3.00 but raised the buying price to about $4.50."}, "b": {"term": "Disgust", "definition": "Lowered both prices: selling about $2.70 versus $4.50 neutral, and buying about $3.00 versus $3.50 neutral."}, "score": 9.9, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "In Lerner and colleagues (2004), participants saw a sad clip of a person dying, a disgust clip of a dirty toilet, or a neutral clip of fish at the Great Barrier Reef, and then set prices for highlighters. The clips were unrelated to the choice, so these were **incidental emotions**, and they changed how much the same object seemed worth.", "children": ["Unrelated clips still changed what the same object was worth."]}, {"text": "**Sadness** lowered the selling price to about **$3.00** but raised the buying price to about **$4.50**. **Disgust** lowered both prices: selling about **$2.70** versus **$4.50** neutral, and buying about **$3.00** versus **$3.50** neutral. It would be a mistake to group the two emotions together, because they differ on the buying price.", "children": ["Sadness raised the buying price, disgust lowered it.", "Different negative emotions can push the same decision in different directions."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Cloudy vs sunny days (Simonsohn, 2007, 2009)", "a": {"term": "Cloudy days", "definition": "Applicants' academic attributes were weighted more heavily, and prospective students were more likely to enroll."}, "b": {"term": "Sunny days", "definition": "Academic attributes were weighted less heavily."}, "score": 9.5, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "The weather is unrelated to the applicants or the school, but it still changed the decision. Incidental emotions include the **surroundings**, like the weather. Simonsohn (2007) analyzed admissions decisions.", "children": ["Weather is an incidental influence on the decision."]}, {"text": "On **cloudy days**, applicants' academic attributes were weighted **more heavily**, while on **sunny days** they were weighted **less heavily**. Simonsohn (2009) found that visitors to a highly rated university were **more likely to enroll** if they came on a cloudy day. These are not decisions about the weather, which is exactly why the effect counts as incidental.", "children": ["Cloudy days increased the weight on academics; sunny days decreased it.", "The weather changed the decision without being part of it."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Human vs computer partner (Sanfey, 2003)", "a": {"term": "Human partner", "definition": "An $8 and $2 offer was accepted about 53 percent of the time, and $9 and $1 about 37 percent."}, "b": {"term": "Computer partner", "definition": "The same offers were accepted about 85 percent and 68 percent."}, "score": 8.9, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "Sanfey and colleagues (2003) had participants play **20 games**, **10 with each partner type**. The offers were identical, and only the partner changed. With a **human partner**, an $8 and $2 offer was accepted about **53 percent** of the time, and $9 and $1 about **37 percent**.", "children": ["Same offers, different partner."]}, {"text": "With a **computer partner**, the same offers were accepted about **85 percent** and **68 percent**. Participants became angry at unfair offers, and they were less angry with an unfair computer. Fair offers were accepted almost every time for both partners.", "children": ["Anger at unfairness is weaker for a computer, so more unfair offers are accepted.", "Fair offers were accepted either way."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Rational responder vs actual responder", "a": {"term": "Rational responder", "definition": "According to utility theory, accept any offer, because even $1 is worth more than nothing."}, "b": {"term": "Actual responders", "definition": "Often rejected low offers, and participants with more right anterior insula activation rejected more, even though rejecting meant getting nothing."}, "score": 6.8, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "According to **utility theory**, a **rational responder** should accept any offer, because even **$1** is worth more than nothing. Actual responders did not behave this way. They often rejected low offers, and participants with more **right anterior insula** activation rejected more, even though rejecting meant getting nothing.", "children": ["Utility theory says accept everything; people do not."]}, {"text": "In the game the proposer gets $10 and offers the responder a split, and if the responder rejects, neither receives anything. In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), fair offers of **$5 and $5** and **$7 and $3** were accepted almost every time, while lower offers were often rejected. **Emotion** is important in decision making, and it can lead people to give up money. **Neuroeconomics** combines psychology, neuroscience and economics to study this.", "children": ["Rejecting is irrational by utility theory, yet common.", "Emotion can make people give up money."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Decay vs interference as read from the Brown-Peterson task", "a": {"term": "Decay account", "definition": "Peterson and Peterson (1959) concluded that information fades from short-term memory over time unless it is rehearsed, because counting backward is so unlike remembering letters that it should not interfere with them."}, "b": {"term": "Interference account", "definition": "Keppel and Underwood (1962) offered the alternative that forgetting comes from other material getting in the way, as when letters from earlier trials compete with the set being tested."}, "score": 9.5, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "Peterson and Peterson (1959) concluded that information **fades from short-term memory over time** unless it is rehearsed. They reasoned that counting backward is so unlike remembering letters that it should not interfere with them. That is the **decay account**.", "children": ["Peterson and Peterson chose decay, with time as the only cause."]}, {"text": "Keppel and Underwood (1962) offered the alternative, the **interference account**. Forgetting comes from other material getting in the way, as when letters from earlier trials compete with the set being tested. Both accounts explain the same result, recall that gets worse the longer the counting lasts, but they predict different things.", "children": ["Interference blames competing material, not time itself."]}, {"text": "Decay depends only on time passing, so the **trial history should not matter**. Interference depends on what else is in memory, so recall at the same delay should be **worse when more earlier sets have piled up**. That is the pattern in the trial-by-trial data, which points to **proactive interference** even though the original authors read the drop as decay.", "children": ["Trial history separates the two accounts.", "The trial-by-trial pattern favors interference."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Heard lists vs read lists (modality effect)", "a": {"term": "Heard lists", "definition": "When items are heard, memory for the last items in the list is better. The advantage also appears for lip-read or mouthed lists."}, "b": {"term": "Read lists", "definition": "When items are only read silently, the memory advantage for the last items is smaller."}, "score": 5.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "When items are **heard**, memory for the **last items** in the list is better, and the advantage also appears for lip-read or mouthed lists. When items are only **read silently**, the advantage for the last items is smaller. For instance, a list spoken aloud is remembered better at its end than the same list read on a screen.", "children": ["Hearing boosts the end of the list more than reading does."]}, {"text": "Neath and Surprenant (2003) tie the effect to extra information that comes with hearing, namely how the list sounded. The finding shaped ideas about **sensory memory**, because the end-of-list advantage seems to rely on a **brief trace of the sound**. The effect concerns the last items of the list, not the whole list.", "children": ["A brief trace of the sound supports the last items.", "The effect is about the end of the list only."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Sound code vs look code (Conrad, 1964; Murray, 1968)", "a": {"term": "Phonological code", "definition": "A speech-based form of the items. If the items are held this way, sound-alike items are confused with one another."}, "b": {"term": "Visual code", "definition": "A memory of how the items look. If the items were held this way, look-alike items would be confused instead."}, "score": 8.6, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "A **phonological code** is a speech-based form of the items, so if items are held this way, **sound-alike** items are confused with one another. A **visual code** is a memory of how items look, so if items were held this way, **look-alike** items would be confused instead.", "children": ["The code you hold items in predicts which items get confused."]}, {"text": "Conrad (1964) and Murray (1968) found that **sound-alike lists were recalled worse**, even when they were read silently. For instance, the letters E and F look alike, but it was similar-sounding letters that were recalled worse. When **articulatory suppression** was applied to visually presented lists, the sound-alike cost disappeared, which shows people had been turning what they saw into a sound code.", "children": ["Sound confusions appeared even for silently read lists.", "Suppression removed the cost, so seen items were recoded as sound."]}, {"text": "What matters is the code used to hold the items, not the format in which they arrived.", "children": ["The holding code matters, not the input format."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Phonological similarity vs neighborhood size", "a": {"term": "Phonological similarity effect", "definition": "Items that sound alike are recalled worse because their sound codes overlap and get confused."}, "b": {"term": "Neighborhood size effect", "definition": "Words that have many similar-sounding neighbors in the language are recalled better, because long-term word knowledge supports the short-term trace."}, "score": 6.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "The **phonological similarity effect** shows sound overlap hurting recall within the list. Items that sound alike are recalled worse because their **sound codes overlap** and get confused.", "children": ["Sound overlap within the list hurts recall."]}, {"text": "The **neighborhood size effect** goes the other way. Words that have many similar-sounding neighbors in the language are recalled **better**, because **long-term word knowledge** supports the short-term trace. For instance, cat is a large-neighborhood word and hippopotamus a small-neighborhood word, and Roodenrys and colleagues (2002) found large-neighborhood words are recalled better in short-term memory tasks.", "children": ["Many neighbors in the language help recall through long-term knowledge."]}, {"text": "Do not treat neighborhood size as another kind of sound confusion, since it is a long-term memory benefit. It also means memory span is not a pure measure of short-term capacity.", "children": ["Neighborhood size is a long-term memory benefit, not confusion."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Framing effect vs default effect", "a": {"term": "Framing effect", "definition": "Choices change with the wording or context of equivalent options. Gain wording leads people to choose the sure option, and loss wording leads them to choose the gamble."}, "b": {"term": "Default effect", "definition": "People tend to choose the option that is already preselected, whatever the wording."}, "score": 8.0, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "The **framing effect** is about how options are described. Choices change with the wording or context of equivalent options: gain wording leads people to choose the **sure option**, and loss wording leads them to choose the **gamble**.", "children": ["Framing changes choices through the description of options."]}, {"text": "The **default effect** is about which option is already chosen. People tend to choose the option that is preselected, whatever the wording. Giuliani and colleagues (2023) found that a **risky default raised risk taking**, a sure default did not raise risk aversion, and the framing and default effects were **additive**. For instance, a risky default raised risk taking on top of the framing effect.", "children": ["The default works on top of the wording, not instead of it."]}, {"text": "People who rated the default as most rational followed it most. That question came after the choice, so it does not show why they chose it.", "children": ["The rationality rating does not explain the choice."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Unconscious attribute substitution vs error sensitivity", "a": {"term": "Unconscious attribute substitution", "definition": "Kahneman and Frederick (2002) explain the error as answering an easier question in place of the one asked, for example by overlooking the words 'more than'."}, "b": {"term": "Error sensitivity", "definition": "Hoover and Healy (2019) prefer this term, because substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause of the error."}, "score": 6.0, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "Kahneman and Frederick (2002) explain the error as **unconscious attribute substitution**. People answer an easier question in place of the one asked, for instance by overlooking the words \"more than.\" This names a cause for the wrong answer.", "children": ["Substitution claims a cause: an easier question replaced the real one."]}, {"text": "Hoover and Healy (2019) prefer the term **error sensitivity**, because substitution cannot be confirmed as the cause. They checked what wrong reasoners remembered about the wording of the bat-and-ball problem (Frederick, 2005). Wrong reasoners **mostly remembered \"more than\"**, so the phrase was not simply overlooked, which counts against the idea that it was dropped.", "children": ["Most wrong reasoners remembered the wording, against the dropped-phrase idea."]}, {"text": "Error sensitivity stays closer to what was observed. It would be a mistake to say the study proved substitution, because it showed only that the cause cannot be confirmed.", "children": ["The study did not prove substitution; it left the cause unconfirmed."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Reward system vs cognitive-control system", "a": {"term": "Reward system", "definition": "The ventral striatum and orbitofrontal cortex. Peers watching can make these regions more active."}, "b": {"term": "Cognitive-control system", "definition": "The lateral prefrontal cortex, which matures slowly and supports controlling impulses."}, "score": 5.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "The **reward system** is the **ventral striatum** and **orbitofrontal cortex**, and peers watching can make these regions more active. The **cognitive-control system** is the **lateral prefrontal cortex**, which matures slowly and supports controlling impulses.", "children": ["Peers can make reward regions more active; the lateral prefrontal cortex supports impulse control."]}, {"text": "In Chein and colleagues (2011), adolescents watched by friends took more risks and showed more reward-region activity, while cognitive-control regions did not change with peers. For instance, an adolescent driving riskily when friends watch is explained by a more active reward system, not a less active control system.", "children": ["Peers raised reward activity without changing cognitive control."]}, {"text": "Albert, Chein and Steinberg (2013) read this as the peer effect working mainly through **reward circuitry** and not through weaker reasoning.", "children": ["Peers push reward up; they do not lower control."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Offloading vs rehearsal", "a": {"term": "Offloading", "definition": "Using actions such as writing things down to reduce the mental load on memory."}, "b": {"term": "Rehearsal", "definition": "Repeating information mentally so that it is not lost."}, "score": 6.0, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Offloading** uses actions such as writing things down to reduce the mental load on memory, so the load sits **outside the head**. **Rehearsal** repeats information mentally so that it is not lost, so the load stays **inside**.", "children": ["Offloading moves the load outside; rehearsal keeps it inside."]}, {"text": "Morrison and Richmond (2020) found that offloading **improved memory**, especially at high loads, and that people offloaded more as the load grew. For instance, when they let people use pen and paper, memory improved most when the list was long. Memory ability did not predict who offloaded, so offloading looks like a strategy used across abilities, not only by people with weak memory.", "children": ["Offloading helps most when the load is high.", "Offloading is not just for people with weak memory."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Verbal vs spatial items under irrelevant speech", "a": {"term": "Verbal items", "definition": "Items that can be named and held in a speech-based code, such as pictured objects."}, "b": {"term": "Spatial items", "definition": "Items defined by where they are, such as dot locations."}, "score": 7.0, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Verbal items** can be named and held in a speech-based code, such as pictured objects. **Spatial items** are defined by where they are, such as dot locations.", "children": ["Verbal items use a speech code, spatial items use location."]}, {"text": "Leist and colleagues (2022) found that ignored speech **hurt serial recall of verbal items but not spatial items**, in both children and adults. For instance, third graders and adults were hurt on the objects but not on the dot locations. That fits **interference with phonological codes** better than accounts based on attention capture or changing-state speech.", "children": ["Ignored speech damaged only verbal recall.", "The pattern fits phonological interference, not general attention capture."]}, {"text": "The speech effect is specific to verbal material, so it does not show a general loss of attention.", "children": ["Not a general attention loss."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Falsifying a rule vs confirming it (Wason and Shapiro, 1971)", "a": {"term": "Falsify", "definition": "Choosing the cases that could show the rule is broken."}, "b": {"term": "Confirm", "definition": "Choosing a case that can only agree with the rule and so cannot test it."}, "score": 7.8, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "To **falsify** a rule is to choose the cases that could show the rule is broken. To **confirm** is to choose a case that can only agree with the rule and so cannot test it. A card that cannot break the rule teaches nothing, however well it fits, because the rule says nothing about what is behind it.", "children": ["Only cases that could break the rule actually test it."]}, {"text": "On the abstract rule, Wason and Shapiro (1971) found most people added a **confirming card** to the correct one, instead of the card that could break the rule. **Thematic rules** were much easier, because relevant experience helps people test them. A choice that agrees with the rule is not a test of it.", "children": ["Abstract rule: most people picked a confirming card.", "Thematic rules were much easier."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Younger vs older children and functional fixedness", "a": {"term": "Older children", "definition": "German and Defeyter (2000) found children aged 6 and 7 were slower after seeing the box used as a container, so one shown use had locked the object in."}, "b": {"term": "Younger children", "definition": "German and Defeyter (2000) found the 5-year-olds were not slowed, and were even faster than the older children after seeing the box as a container."}, "score": 6.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "German and Defeyter (2000) first showed children a box used as a container and then measured how fast they solved the problem. **Older children**, aged **6 and 7**, were **slower** afterward, so one shown use had locked the object in.", "children": ["For 6 and 7 year olds, one shown use slowed them down."]}, {"text": "The **5-year-olds** were not slowed, and were even **faster** than the older children after seeing the box as a container. The results suggest younger children have a **more fluid idea of function**, so one shown use did not lock the object in. This is **immunity to functional fixedness** in young children, though the finding is about speed after being shown a use, so it does not say young children never show fixedness.", "children": ["Young children were not slowed by the shown use.", "The claim is about speed after a shown use, not never being fixed."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Staying vs switching in the Monty Hall problem", "a": {"term": "Staying", "definition": "Keeping the first choice keeps only the chance that the first guess was right, which is the smaller share."}, "b": {"term": "Switching", "definition": "Switching wins the larger share, because the first guess was probably wrong and the revealed option removes a wrong one."}, "score": 5.0, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Staying** keeps the first choice, which keeps only the chance that the first guess was right, the **smaller share**. **Switching** wins the **larger share**, because the first guess was probably wrong and the revealed option removes a wrong one.", "children": ["Staying keeps the small original chance, switching wins the larger share."]}, {"text": "The first pick keeps its original chance, so the remaining chance moves to the one other option left. After the first choice, a losing option is revealed, and switching then wins more often than staying. Many people think it makes no difference, which is the intuition the problem is used to show is mistaken. It would be a mistake to reason that two options left means equal chances.", "children": ["The revealed option shifts the leftover chance to the other door.", "Two options left does not mean equal chances."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Percent correct vs the pattern of position errors", "a": {"term": "Percent correct", "definition": "Counts only how many items were placed in exactly the right position."}, "b": {"term": "Position error pattern", "definition": "Looks at where the wrong items went. Items were usually placed correctly, and errors were systematic, with the item landing in an adjacent position."}, "score": 4.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Percent correct** counts only how many items were placed in exactly the right position. An item one place off counts as completely wrong, even though it still shows partial knowledge of its position.", "children": ["Percent correct scores a near miss as a total failure."]}, {"text": "The **position error pattern** looks at where the wrong items went. Items were usually placed correctly, and errors were **systematic**, with the item landing in an **adjacent position**. People keep approximate position information even when they miss the exact one, and a percent-correct score hides this.", "children": ["Errors cluster near the true position.", "Look at which errors were made, not only how many."]}]}, {"module": 14, "title": "Self-report of imagery vs an imagery task (Shepard and Metzler, 1971)", "a": {"term": "Self-report of imagery", "definition": "Asking people to describe their mental images is unreliable, because you cannot tell whether the images or only the descriptions differ."}, "b": {"term": "An imagery task with a varied critical part", "definition": "Shepard and Metzler (1971) had people judge whether two block shapes were the same or mirror images when one was turned."}, "score": 9.0, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "Asking people to describe their mental images, a **self-report of imagery**, is unreliable, because you cannot tell whether the images or only the descriptions differ. An **imagery task with a varied critical part** avoids this problem.", "children": ["Self-report cannot separate real image differences from description differences."]}, {"text": "Shepard and Metzler (1971) had people judge whether two block shapes were the same or mirror images when one was turned. The **larger the turn**, the **longer the response**, at a **constant rate**. This suggests mental images are much like real images, because people **mentally rotate** them. The evidence is the pattern of response times, not what people say they see.", "children": ["Response time rose steadily with the size of the turn.", "Rotation timing is evidence for image-like representations."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Validity vs believability (belief bias and mental models)", "a": {"term": "Validity", "definition": "Asks whether the conclusion must follow if the premises are true. It depends on the form of the argument and not on what the words say."}, "b": {"term": "Believability", "definition": "Asks whether the conclusion sounds true given what you know. Belief bias is judging a syllogism valid because its conclusion is believable."}, "score": 7.0, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Validity** asks whether the conclusion must follow if the premises are true, so it depends on the **form** of the argument and not on what the words say. **Believability** asks whether the conclusion sounds true given what you know. These are separate questions.", "children": ["Validity is about form, believability is about what you know."]}, {"text": "**Belief bias** is answering the second question when asked the first, judging a syllogism valid because its conclusion is believable. **71 percent** judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid. For instance, \"All of the students are tired, some tired people are irritable, so some of the students are irritable\" seems reasonable but is invalid. A believable conclusion does not make a syllogism valid, and an unbelievable one does not make it invalid.", "children": ["Belief bias judges validity by believability.", "71 percent called believable but invalid syllogisms valid."]}, {"text": "The **mental model** approach (Johnson-Laird, 1999) is the safeguard. You build a model of the premises, form a tentative conclusion, then search for an exception where the premises hold but the conclusion fails. The conclusion is valid only if **no such model exists**.", "children": ["Search for a counterexample model; valid only if none exists."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Short-term memory vs long-term memory", "a": {"term": "Short-term memory", "definition": "Holds about five to seven items for roughly fifteen to twenty seconds and is easily disrupted by interruption and interference."}, "b": {"term": "Long-term memory", "definition": "Retains large amounts of information for years or even decades."}, "score": 8.9, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Short-term memory** holds about **five to seven items** for roughly **fifteen to twenty seconds** and is easily disrupted by interruption and interference. **Long-term memory** retains large amounts of information for **years or even decades**. They differ in capacity and in duration, so it would be a mistake to treat long-term memory as simply more of short-term memory.", "children": ["Short-term memory is small and brief, and long-term memory is large and lasting."]}, {"text": "Rehearsal moves information from short-term toward long-term memory, and retrieval moves it back, so the flow goes both ways and not only toward storage. For instance, you look up a phone number, repeat it to keep it in short-term memory, and later retrieve it from long-term memory back into short-term memory to dial it.", "children": ["Rehearsal moves information toward long-term memory, and retrieval moves it back."]}, {"text": "Short-term memory is the active window on the present, and long-term memory is the large body of knowledge stored outside current awareness.", "children": ["One is the current window, and the other is the stored knowledge."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Declarative vs procedural memory", "a": {"term": "Declarative memory", "definition": "Long-term memory for events and knowledge, with two branches: episodic memory for events and semantic memory for facts."}, "b": {"term": "Procedural memory", "definition": "Long-term memory for skills."}, "score": 4.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Declarative memory** is long-term memory for events and knowledge, with two branches: **episodic memory** for events and **semantic memory** for facts. **Procedural memory** is long-term memory for **skills**. Episodic and semantic memory are both inside declarative memory.", "children": ["Declarative covers events and facts, and procedural covers skills."]}, {"text": "The memory tree sorts long-term memory by **function** and not by how long things last. Declarative memory is what you can state, and procedural memory is what you can do.", "children": ["Declarative is knowing that, and procedural is knowing how."]}, {"text": "For instance, knowing what a bicycle is is declarative, and knowing how to ride one is procedural.", "children": ["The concept is stated, and the skill is performed."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Encoding, storage and retrieval failure", "a": {"term": "Encoding and storage", "definition": "Encoding converts information into a form that can be stored, and storage keeps it."}, "b": {"term": "Retrieval", "definition": "Bringing stored information back out when it is needed."}, "score": 6.7, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Encoding** converts information into a form that can be stored, and **storage** keeps it. **Retrieval** is bringing stored information back out when it is needed. A failure to remember could come from a problem at any of the three operations.", "children": ["Memory depends on three operations, and any one can fail."]}, {"text": "Forgetting on its own does not show which one failed. For instance, forgetting a familiar name could mean it was never encoded well, was lost from storage, or cannot be retrieved right now. It would be a mistake to name the failed stage from the failure alone.", "children": ["The failure alone does not identify the stage."]}, {"text": "Memory is also an active system that can organize and even alter information, so it is not a recording kept unchanged.", "children": ["Memory can organize and alter information, so it is not an unchanged recording."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Simple vs complex objects in visual short-term memory", "a": {"term": "Simple objects", "definition": "Objects such as colored squares need little information to represent, so more of them can be held."}, "b": {"term": "Complex objects", "definition": "Objects such as polygons, shaded cubes or Chinese-character-like forms need more information to represent, so fewer can be held."}, "score": 8.8, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "**Simple objects**, such as colored squares, need little information to represent, so more of them can be held. **Complex objects**, such as polygons, shaded cubes or Chinese-character-like forms, need more information to represent, so fewer can be held.", "children": ["The more information an object needs, the fewer objects fit."]}, {"text": "**Alvarez and Cavanagh (2004)** found that same-different performance depends on how complex the stimuli are. In the change-detection task, colored squares supported a higher estimated capacity than shaded cubes. Capacity is therefore constrained by the amount of information needed to represent the objects.", "children": ["Estimated capacity shrinks as complexity rises."]}, {"text": "A capacity for one category of stimulus is not a universal estimate for all visual objects. Do not count every object as an equally sized item.", "children": ["Capacity depends on object complexity and is not one fixed number."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Triangle problem vs chain problem", "a": {"term": "Triangle problem", "definition": "Ten dots form an upward-pointing triangle, and you must move exactly three dots to make it point down, with seven staying in place."}, "b": {"term": "Chain problem", "definition": "Four short sections of chain must become one closed loop, where opening a link costs 2 cents, closing it costs 3 cents and the budget is 15 cents."}, "score": 9.8, "tier": "high", "blocks": [{"text": "In the **triangle problem**, ten dots form an upward-pointing triangle, and you must **move exactly three dots** to make it point down, with seven staying in place. In the **chain problem**, four short sections of chain must become one closed loop, where **opening a link costs 2 cents**, **closing it costs 3 cents** and the budget is **15 cents**.", "children": ["Both are puzzles with a tight constraint and an unobvious solution."]}, {"text": "Both are **insight problems** used by **Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987)**, and both are solved by a change in representation. In the triangle, the top dot becomes a new bottom point. In the chain, one three-link section is opened into separate links that join the other three sections, and three open-and-close operations use the full 15 cents.", "children": ["Each is solved by seeing the pieces in a new way."]}, {"text": "Metcalfe and Wiebe (1987) compared both with algebra and found that warmth **jumped late** for them, from about **3 to 7**, unlike the gradual rise for algebra.", "children": ["Late warmth jumps mark both as insight problems."]}]}, {"module": 10, "title": "Problem vs knowing the goal", "a": {"term": "A problem", "definition": "A situation with a present state, a goal and an obstacle between them, where the solution is not immediately obvious."}, "b": {"term": "Stating the goal", "definition": "Being able to describe exactly what the finished situation looks like."}, "score": 1.4, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "A **problem** is a situation with a **present state**, a **goal** and an **obstacle** between them, where the solution is not immediately obvious. **Stating the goal** is only being able to describe exactly what the finished situation looks like.", "children": ["A problem needs an obstacle, and describing the goal does not remove it."]}, {"text": "The difficulty is the route and not whether the goal has been stated. In the **numbered-tile puzzle**, for instance, the initial state is 8, empty, 6 over 5, 4, 7 over 2, 3, 1, and the goal is empty, 1, 2 over 3, 4, 5 over 6, 7, 8. You can describe that goal arrangement exactly and still not know the sequence of moves that gets there.", "children": ["Knowing the goal is not knowing the moves."]}, {"text": "It would be a mistake to think a tile being on the board means it is in its required position, because the **whole arrangement defines the state**.", "children": ["A state is the full arrangement, not any single tile."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Russian marriage problem vs mutilated checkerboard", "a": {"term": "Russian marriage problem (source)", "definition": "There are 32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women. After two bachelors die, 30 men and 32 women remain and the question is whether 31 heterosexual marriages can be formed. The answer is no."}, "b": {"term": "Mutilated checkerboard (target)", "definition": "Removing two opposite corners leaves 62 squares, which matches the area of 31 dominoes, but the two removed squares are the same color so the covering is impossible."}, "score": 7.0, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "In the **Russian marriage problem**, the source, there are **32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women**. After two bachelors die, 30 men and 32 women remain and the question is whether **31 heterosexual marriages** can be formed. The answer is **no**.", "children": ["The source problem shows that unequal groups cannot be fully paired."]}, {"text": "In the **mutilated checkerboard**, the target, removing two opposite corners leaves **62 squares**, which matches the area of 31 dominoes. But the two removed squares are the same color, so the covering is impossible.", "children": ["The target has the same hidden imbalance."]}, {"text": "**Marriages correspond to dominoes**, the two groups of people correspond to the two square colors, and removing two bachelors corresponds to removing two same-colored squares. A valid marriage needs one member of each group, just as a domino needs one square of each color. Dividing 62 by two gives 31 pairs by total size but ignores which kinds each pair must contain.", "children": ["The relationship among the elements transfers, not their identical appearance."]}]}, {"module": 11, "title": "Having an idea vs creative problem solving", "a": {"term": "Having an idea", "definition": "Generating a novel idea or new connection. Divergent thinking is open-ended and allows many potential solutions."}, "b": {"term": "Creative problem solving", "definition": "A fuller process that includes problem generation, formulation, solving with evaluation and planning, and solution implementation, usually after a lengthy period of trial and error."}, "score": 4.7, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Having an idea** is generating a novel idea or new connection. It rests on **divergent thinking**, which is open-ended and allows many potential solutions. Ideas are necessary, since without them the process stops.", "children": ["An idea is the raw material of creativity."]}, {"text": "**Creative problem solving** is a fuller process that includes **problem generation, formulation, solving with evaluation and planning, and solution implementation**, usually after a lengthy period of trial and error. Generating ideas does not evaluate, plan or carry out a response.", "children": ["Creative problem solving covers the whole path from finding a problem to acting on a solution."]}, {"text": "Finding the problem, defining it and finding ideas come before evaluating and acting on them. Creativity is more than getting an idea.", "children": ["Ideas are one stage of the process, not the whole of it."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Easy to recall vs actually frequent (availability)", "a": {"term": "Easy to bring to mind", "definition": "Events or words that are distinctive, emotionally salient or retrieved easily, which makes them feel more numerous."}, "b": {"term": "Actually frequent", "definition": "How often the event or word really occurs."}, "score": 6.7, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "Events or words that are **distinctive, emotionally salient or retrieved easily** feel more numerous. **Actual frequency** is how often the event or word really occurs. The availability heuristic goes wrong when the first is mistaken for the second.", "children": ["Ease of recall is a feeling, and frequency is a fact."]}, {"text": "About **70 percent** say English has more words beginning with r than words with r as the third letter, but **r-third words are about three times more common**. Retrieving words by first letter is easier, so those words feel more numerous.", "children": ["The retrieval route, not the real count, drives the judgment."]}, {"text": "A death from **botulism** can be front-page news while **asthma** deaths go virtually unnoticed, so people pick the less likely cause. Memorability is not frequency.", "children": ["News coverage shapes what feels likely."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Illusory correlation vs a real association", "a": {"term": "Illusory correlation", "definition": "A relationship that appears to exist even though it does not exist or is much weaker than assumed."}, "b": {"term": "Real association", "definition": "A relationship that is actually present in the data."}, "score": 4.4, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "An **illusory correlation** is a relationship that appears to exist even though it does not exist or is **much weaker than assumed**. A **real association** is a relationship actually present in the data.", "children": ["The illusion is a belief in a link the data do not support."]}, {"text": "**Stereotypes direct attention** to behaviors that fit the expected description, so a stereotype feels confirmed because the fitting instances were noticed and retained. Stereotype-consistent behaviors are easier to bring to mind, and their **availability is mistaken for evidence** that the behavior is strongly associated with the group.", "children": ["Noticing and remembering only the fitting cases makes the link feel real."]}, {"text": "The apparent evidence is shaped by what was noticed and remembered.", "children": ["The evidence looks strong because of selective attention."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Feared event vs actual risk (after September 11)", "a": {"term": "Emotional impact", "definition": "The fear produced by the attacks led to a decision based on how the event felt."}, "b": {"term": "Relative probability", "definition": "The probability of dying in a car accident is much greater than while flying."}, "score": 1.4, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "A decision can rest on **emotional impact** or on **relative probability**. The fear produced by the attacks led to a decision based on how the event felt, even though the probability of dying in a car accident is **much greater** than while flying.", "children": ["Fear tracks how an event feels, and risk tracks how likely it is."]}, {"text": "After September 11, **air travel decreased and driving increased**. The reported number of Americans who died on the road while avoiding flying **exceeded the total number of passengers killed on the four hijacked flights**.", "children": ["Avoiding the feared event increased exposure to a more likely danger."]}, {"text": "Emotional impact and probability are different bases for a decision, and only one of them matches the actual risk.", "children": ["Feeling safe is not the same as being safe."]}]}, {"module": 12, "title": "Decision vs judgment vs reasoning", "a": {"term": "Decision", "definition": "The process of choosing between alternatives."}, "b": {"term": "Judgment and reasoning", "definition": "Reasoning is the process of drawing conclusions, and decisions are based on the judgments people make."}, "score": 3.6, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "A **decision** is the process of **choosing between alternatives**. **Reasoning** is the process of **drawing conclusions**, and decisions are based on the **judgments** people make.", "children": ["Reasoning produces judgments, and judgments feed decisions."]}, {"text": "**Inductive reasoning**, from observations to conclusions that are probably but not definitely true, is one of the primary mechanisms involved in making judgments. In everyday life people use past experience to predict what will happen, especially in familiar situations.", "children": ["Induction turns past experience into the estimates that judgments are made of."]}, {"text": "A decision is a choice, and a judgment is an estimate that feeds it.", "children": ["Estimate first, then choose."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Context of a decision (cesarean case)", "a": {"term": "Seen alone", "definition": "Physicians read the hypothetical cesarean test case by itself as the control."}, "b": {"term": "Seen after other cases", "definition": "Physicians read the same case after four cases with serious complications, or after four fairly routine cases."}, "score": 7.7, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "In the cesarean case, some physicians read the hypothetical test case **by itself** as the control. Others read the same case **after four cases** with serious complications, or after four fairly routine cases.", "children": ["The identical patient was seen alone or after other cases."]}, {"text": "**Significantly more doctors** recommended cesarean after reading the non-serious cases first. The cases just considered changed how the same patient was judged. Decisions depend on **context** and on how choices are presented.", "children": ["Prior cases changed the judgment of an identical patient.", "Context shapes decisions."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Emotion as a help vs a hindrance in decisions", "a": {"term": "Emotion that helps", "definition": "People with damage to an area of the prefrontal cortex who have flattened emotions have impaired decision making, so emotion takes part in decisions."}, "b": {"term": "Emotion in excess", "definition": "Anxious people tend toward risk avoidance, and optimistic people ignore negative information, which in excess can lead to poor decisions."}, "score": 4.5, "tier": "low", "blocks": [{"text": "**Emotion helps** decision making in a basic way. People with damage to an area of the prefrontal cortex who have **flattened emotions** have **impaired decision making**, so emotion takes part in decisions.", "children": ["Losing emotion impairs decisions, so emotion is part of deciding."]}, {"text": "**Emotion in excess** can lead to poor decisions. **Anxious people** tend toward risk avoidance, such as passing up choices that could lead to large negative consequences, and **optimistic people** ignore negative information. Emotions take part in decision making and do not only get in its way, and whether they help depends on how they push the choice. Emotion is not simply the opposite of good decision making.", "children": ["Too much anxiety or optimism distorts choices.", "Emotion helps or hurts depending on how it pushes the choice."]}]}, {"module": 13, "title": "Categorical vs conditional syllogism", "a": {"term": "Categorical syllogism", "definition": "Describes the relation between two categories using all, no or some, such as All birds are animals, All animals eat food, therefore all birds eat food."}, "b": {"term": "Conditional syllogism", "definition": "Has a first premise of the form If p, then q, where p is the condition and q is what follows."}, "score": 7.0, "tier": "mid", "blocks": [{"text": "Both kinds of syllogism have **two premises and a conclusion**, and Aristotle introduced the syllogism. A **categorical syllogism** describes the relation between two categories using **all, no or some**, such as \"All birds are animals, all animals eat food, therefore all birds eat food.\"", "children": ["Both have two premises and a conclusion; categorical uses all, no or some."]}, {"text": "A **conditional syllogism** has a first premise of the form **if p, then q**, where p is the condition and q is what follows. For instance, \"If I study I'll get a good grade\" is a conditional first premise. The conditional kind has **four types** that differ in the second premise, **two valid and two not valid**. A syllogism can be invalid even when every statement seems reasonable.", "children": ["Conditional syllogisms start from if p then q.", "Four types: two valid, two not valid."]}]}];
